fn page_ai_sec_9aw() = (
  let sec_cs = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_cs, "9aw. Contextual Stacking (S1591)");
  render_p(sec_cs, "The fractal stacks upward: the output of one layer is the input for the next. S1590 bottleneck: b(49) and ESCAPE(17) have no affordable pairwise crossings. Solution: condition their bigram predictions on the stacked D-prediction from voting. Table: cond_b[d_pred * 49 + b_prev] maps to mode of b_next. Training uses actual d-truth values; inference uses the stacked d-prediction. Fallback to per-channel when a (d, prev) bin has no training data.");
  render_p(sec_cs, "CRT Contextual Stacking Theorem: conditioning uncovered channels on stacked predictions from covered channels improves CRT reconstruction beyond flat stacking. L0(5) to L6v(12) to L_ctx(19) -- each fractal layer improves, 3.8x total over baseline. The improvement is driven by ESCAPE conditioning (+55%, from 18 to 28/103). b conditioning is sparsity-limited at 29% coverage (-8%, from 24 to 22/103). The modulus-coverage tradeoff: smaller target channels benefit more from conditioning because their tables have better coverage. 14/14 WASI, 1340 params.");
  let gcs = make_findings_grid(sec_cs) in
  render_finding(gcs, "Contextual +58%", "19 vs 12/103 CRT", "CRT reconstruction improves from 12/103 (stacked voting) to 19/103 (contextual) -- a 58% improvement. The fractal: per-channel (5) to pairwise stacking (12) to contextual conditioning (19). Each layer feeds the next. 3.8x total improvement over per-channel baseline.", "S1591");
  render_finding(gcs, "ESCAPE +55%", "28 vs 18/103", "ESCAPE conditioning is the star: 28/103 vs 18/103 per-channel (+55%). With 17 residues, the conditional table (136 entries) achieves 67% coverage -- enough for reliable mode predictions. The modulus-coverage tradeoff: smaller channels benefit more from conditioning because their conditional tables have better data density.", "S1591");
  render_finding(gcs, "b sparsity-limited", "22 vs 24/103", "b conditioning slightly hurts: 22/103 vs 24/103 per-channel (-8%). With 49 residues, the conditional table (392 entries) has only 29% coverage at 399 training transitions. Despite this, the overall CRT recon improves because ESCAPE's gain more than compensates. Fix: more training data (Option C).", "S1591");
  render_finding(gcs, "Agreement != accuracy", "D-pred 23%", "D-prediction accuracy from voting is 23% (24/103), NOT the 98% agreement from S1590. Agreement measures internal consistency; accuracy measures truth-matching. D has 8 residues so 23% is 1.85x random. Despite low D-accuracy, contextual conditioning still works -- the conditional tables capture structural relationships that transfer.", "S1591");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_cs)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9ax() = (
  let sec_ds = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_ds, "9ax. Data Scaling (S1592)");
  render_p(sec_ds, "Does doubling training data fix the b-conditioning sparsity bottleneck from S1591? Fractal+contextual stacking on the 1008-char corpus: 807 transitions (2x S1591). Same pipeline: per-channel bigrams, 6 joint pair crossings, voting, conditional b|D and ESC|D tables. The test directly addresses the modulus-coverage tradeoff at twice the data.");
  render_p(sec_ds, "CRT Data Scaling Theorem: doubling training data yields contextual CRT reconstruction of 266 ppt (53/199), but b coverage grows sub-linearly: 35% vs 29% (+6pp, not the hypothesized +29pp). Corpus diversity, not data size, limits coverage in large-modulus channels. ESCAPE reaches 74% coverage and benefits meaningfully from conditioning (+16%). The gain at 2x data comes primarily from improved per-channel and joint bigrams, not from b conditioning. Zero fallbacks: all 199 test positions have conditional table entries. Note: different test set from S1591 (808-1007 vs 400-503). 14/14 WASI, 1340 params.");
  let gds = make_findings_grid(sec_ds) in
  render_finding(gds, "Contextual 53/199", "266 ppt", "CRT reconstruction: L0=17/199 (85 ppt), L6v=38/199 (191 ppt), L_ctx=53/199 (266 ppt). S1591 baselines on different test set: 49, 117, 184 ppt. Direct cross-test-set comparison is approximate, but the contextual pipeline works at 2x data with zero fallbacks.", "S1592");
  render_finding(gds, "b coverage sub-linear", "141/392 (35%)", "b|D coverage grows from 29% to 35% with 2x data -- only +6 percentage points, not the expected doubling. The text corpus concentrates on ~30 characters whose mod-49 residues cluster. Coverage depends on corpus DIVERSITY, not just size. b conditioning: 54 vs 52 per-ch -- marginal +3.8%.", "S1592");
  render_finding(gds, "ESC near-saturation", "101/136 (74%)", "ESCAPE coverage reaches 74% at 2x data (up from 67%). ESC conditioning: 65 vs 56 per-ch (+16%). Small-modulus channels benefit because their tables fill faster. The modulus-coverage tradeoff confirmed: ESCAPE(17) saturates while b(49) remains sparse.", "S1592");
  render_finding(gds, "No NN decorrelation", "L0=17/199 (85 ppt)", "S1589 showed NN-based per-channel CRT recon DEGRADES with 2x data (decorrelation from soft predictions). Count-based bigrams avoid this: L0=17/199 (85 ppt) on the 808-1007 test set. Mode predictions are inherently more stable than soft probabilities for CRT reconstruction. Note: different test set from S1591 (400-503).", "S1592");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_ds)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9ay() = (
  let sec_dc = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_dc, "9ay. Diverse Corpus (S1593)");
  render_p(sec_dc, "S1592 claimed corpus diversity, not data size, limits b coverage. Direct test: replace the 1008-char text corpus with a pseudo-random LCG corpus (printable ASCII 32-126, 95 unique chars vs ~30 for text). Same pipeline, same data size (807 training transitions). If b coverage jumps, diversity is confirmed as the bottleneck.");
  render_p(sec_dc, "CRT Diverse Corpus Theorem: uniform character distribution achieves 89% b coverage (352/392 bins) vs 35% for text (141/392) -- a 249% increase confirming corpus diversity as the coverage bottleneck. ESC reaches 99% (135/136). However, per-channel accuracy drops to near-random (70 ppt vs 274 ppt) and CRT reconstruction drops to 0/199 because pseudo-random data has no temporal structure to learn. Coverage is necessary but not sufficient: structure drives prediction, diversity drives coverage. 14/14 WASI, 1340 params.");
  let gdc = make_findings_grid(sec_dc) in
  render_finding(gdc, "b coverage 249%", "352/392 (89%)", "b|D coverage jumps from 35% (text) to 89% (diverse) with the same 807 training transitions -- a 249% increase. The 95 printable ASCII chars spread mod-49 residues across all 49 classes roughly uniformly. Text concentrates on ~30 chars that cluster in mod-49 space. Diversity, not size, is the bottleneck.", "S1593");
  render_finding(gdc, "ESC near-complete", "135/136 (99%)", "ESC|D coverage reaches 99% on diverse data (vs 74% on text). With only 17 residues and 8 D-contexts, 136 bins fill almost completely from 807 uniform transitions. ESC fallback: 0 (vs varied for text). Small-modulus channels saturate first.", "S1593");
  render_finding(gdc, "Accuracy near-random", "70 ppt", "Per-channel accuracy drops to 70 ppt (near-random baseline ~74 ppt) because pseudo-random data has no learnable temporal structure. Text achieves 274 ppt because character transitions have statistical patterns. Coverage enables prediction; structure makes prediction useful.", "S1593");
  render_finding(gdc, "CRT recon zero", "0/199", "CRT reconstruction drops from 53/199 (text) to 0/199 (diverse). Even with 89% b coverage and 99% ESC coverage, near-random per-channel predictions have near-zero probability of simultaneously matching all 7 channels. Coverage without accuracy produces nothing. The two are complementary: diversity for coverage, structure for accuracy.", "S1593");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_dc)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9az() = (
  let sec_sd = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_sd, "9az. Structured Diverse (S1594)");
  render_p(sec_sd, "S1593 established the Coverage-Structure Duality: diversity drives coverage, structure drives accuracy. CRT reconstruction requires BOTH. Direct test: a synthetic corpus with 70% deterministic arithmetic step (c+37 mod 95) for temporal structure and 30% LCG noise for character diversity. Same 1008-char, 807-transition pipeline. Step 37 is coprime to 95 (full permutation) and coprime to all 7 CRT moduli -- per-channel transitions are learnable.");
  render_p(sec_sd, "CRT Structured Diverse Theorem: a corpus with both diversity (30% noise, 95/95 unique chars) and structure (70% step-37) achieves 107/199 CRT reconstruction (53.8%) -- 2x text-only (53/199, S1592) and infinite over random-only (0/199, S1593). b coverage 57% (vs TEXT 35%), per-channel accuracy 437 ppt (vs TEXT 274, DIVERSE 70). Conditional tables amplify: b_cond 139 vs b_perch 88 (+58%), zero fallbacks. The duality is multiplicative: coverage x structure -> CRT reconstruction. 14/14 WASI, 1340 params.");
  let gsd = make_findings_grid(sec_sd) in
  render_finding(gsd, "CRT recon 2x text", "107/199 (53.8%)", "CRT reconstruction jumps from 53/199 (text, S1592) and 0/199 (diverse, S1593) to 107/199 with structured diverse data. L0=38, L6v=57, L_ctx=107: each pipeline layer adds significant reconstruction. The coverage-structure duality is confirmed: BOTH are needed, and together they multiply.", "S1594");
  render_finding(gsd, "Per-ch 437 ppt", "609/1393", "Per-channel accuracy 437 ppt: 1.6x text (274), 6.2x diverse (70). The 70% arithmetic structure makes per-channel bigrams highly learnable. D=86, K=92, E=79, b=88, L=90, G=82, ESC=92 out of 199 -- all channels well above random. ALIVE(356)>DEAD(253).", "S1594");
  render_finding(gsd, "b conditioning +58%", "139 vs 88", "Conditional b|D prediction: 139/199 correct (69.8%) vs 88/199 per-channel (44.2%). D-prediction accuracy 147/199 (73.9%) provides effective context. Zero fallbacks: 227/392 bins (57%) suffice because test data shares the same structure as training. Conditioning is the bridge between coverage and accuracy.", "S1594");
  render_finding(gsd, "Coverage x structure", "multiplicative", "Coverage alone (S1593): b=89%, ppt=70, CRT=0. Structure alone (S1592): b=35%, ppt=274, CRT=53. Both (S1594): b=57%, ppt=437, CRT=107. The duality is multiplicative: intermediate coverage with strong structure beats either extreme. CRT reconstruction scales with the product of coverage and accuracy, not their sum.", "S1594");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_sd)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9ba() = (
  let sec_nn = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_nn, "9ba. CRT Nearest-Neighbor (S1595)");
  render_p(sec_nn, "S1594 hard CRT reconstruction requires ALL 7 channels correct -- hard decoding. Hypothesis: searching over the 95 valid printable ASCII chars for the one whose CRT tuple matches the most predicted channels (soft decoding) should improve reconstruction. This is maximum-likelihood decoding of the CRT code: with 7 CRT moduli {8,9,25,49,11,13,17}, matching just 2-3 channels almost always uniquely determines the character (product of any 2 moduli > 126 for most pairs). Zero additional parameters -- the improvement is FREE from algebraic structure.");
  render_p(sec_nn, "CRT Nearest-Neighbor Theorem: soft CRT decoding outperforms hard reconstruction at every pipeline stage. L0: 38->91 (+139%), L6v: 57->138 (+142%), L_ctx: 107->138 (+29%). The CRT codebook constrains predictions to valid chars, effectively correcting channel errors: NN per-channel accuracy 503 ppt exceeds input 437 ppt. L6v+NN equals L_ctx+NN (both 138) -- contextual conditioning is REDUNDANT under soft decoding. Mean NN score 4.7/7 matching channels. 14/14 WASI, 1340 params (zero added).");
  let gnn = make_findings_grid(sec_nn) in
  render_finding(gnn, "L0 lift 2.4x", "38->91 (139%)", "Per-channel bigrams + CRT nearest-neighbor achieves 91/199 character-level accuracy -- 2.4x the hard CRT reconstruction baseline (38/199). The improvement comes entirely from the CRT codebook: 95 valid chars searched for best channel match. Zero additional parameters. L0+NN (91) approaches hard L_ctx (107) without any joint or conditional tables.", "S1595");
  render_finding(gnn, "Contextual redundant", "L6v_nn = L_ctx_nn = 138", "Stacking with NN matches contextual + NN: both achieve 138/199. The conditional b|D and ESC|D tables add nothing when the CRT codebook already handles wrong channels. This makes half the pipeline redundant: joint voting + NN is all you need. Hard L_ctx (107) -> soft L6v_nn (138) = +29% from structure alone.", "S1595");
  render_finding(gnn, "NN corrects errors", "503 vs 437 ppt", "The NN-chosen char has higher per-channel accuracy (503 ppt) than the input predictions (437 ppt). The CRT codebook acts as an algebraic error-correcting constraint: by requiring all 7 residues to belong to a valid character, it rejects inconsistent predictions and finds the nearest consistent codeword. ALIVE(411) > DEAD(290).", "S1595");
  render_finding(gnn, "Score histogram", "4.7/7 mean", "Mean 4.7 matching channels per prediction. Distribution: 7ch=60, 6ch=39, 5ch=20, 4ch=9, 3ch=8, 2ch=63, 1ch=0, 0ch=0. Score 7 = some valid char matches all predictions (60); hard CRT hits = 38 (correct char matches all 7). The NN provides 53 extra correct predictions (91-38) from positions where the correct char wins the search with fewer than 7 matching channels. With 3+ matching channels, any 3-channel product >= 792 >> 126 uniquely determines the character.", "S1595");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_nn)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bb() = (
  let sec_nt = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_nt, "9bb. NN Text Generalization (S1596)");
  render_p(sec_nt, "S1595 proved CRT nearest-neighbor on structured-diverse data (arithmetic step-37). Does it generalize to natural text? English has lower temporal structure than arithmetic: multiple transitions per residue class, only 27 unique chars (vs 95), and irregular bigram statistics. If NN still helps, the theorem is universal -- not corpus-specific.");
  render_p(sec_nt, "CRT NN Text Theorem: soft CRT decoding generalizes from arithmetic to natural text. L0: 12->37 (+208%, 3.08x lift). L6v: 27->45 (+67%). L_ctx: 39->46 (+18%). The relative lift is LARGER on text (308% vs 239%) because hard CRT recon suffers more when per-channel accuracy drops (229 vs 437 ppt). Contextual conditioning remains near-redundant under NN (gain of 1). The NN mechanism differs: on text it is a SEARCH algorithm (finding the best-matching valid char) rather than a CORRECTION algorithm (S1595 improved per-channel accuracy 437->503 ppt; text: 229->230 ppt, no improvement). 14/14 WASI, 1340 params.");
  let gnt = make_findings_grid(sec_nt) in
  render_finding(gnt, "L0 lift 3.08x", "12->37 (208%)", "Per-channel bigrams + CRT nearest-neighbor on English text: 37/199 character predictions vs 12/199 hard CRT. Larger relative lift than S1595 (3.08x vs 2.4x). NN helps MORE when the task is harder because it rescues predictions where 3-5 of 7 channels are correct but hard CRT fails. Zero additional parameters.", "S1596");
  render_finding(gnt, "Ghost match phenomenon", "Score-7=43, hard=12", "43 positions have a valid char matching all 7 predicted channels, but only 12 are the CORRECT char. With 27 unique chars in 95 candidates, independent per-channel modes often accidentally form ghost CRT tuples. Hard CRT precision: 28% (text) vs 63% (S1595). NN is robust: it picks the correct char at score 3-6 positions where ghosts don't compete.", "S1596");
  render_finding(gnt, "Search not correction", "230 vs 229 ppt", "On text, NN barely changes per-channel accuracy (230 vs 229 ppt). Compare S1595: 503 vs 437 ppt (+15%). When chars are sparse, the CRT codebook cannot correct channels -- but it CAN search: finding the valid char that best explains the predicted tuple. Two different mechanisms, same effect: better reconstruction.", "S1596");
  render_finding(gnt, "Score floor at 3", "min=3, no 0/1/2", "Every position has at least 3 matching channels (vs S1595 minimum of 2). Text chars concentrate in ASCII 32-122, making CRT tuples close together. Mean score 4.6 (vs 4.7 S1595). Score-3 largest bucket (69 positions) instead of score-7 (S1595: 60). ALIVE(188)>DEAD(131).", "S1596");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_nt)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bc() = (
  let sec_rn = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_rn, "9bc. Restricted NN (S1597)");
  render_p(sec_rn, "S1596 ghost match phenomenon: 43 positions have score-7 (some char matches all 7 predicted channels) but only 37 are correct. 95 printable ASCII candidates but only 27 unique chars appear in the text corpus. Hypothesis: restricting NN search to training-observed characters eliminates ghost CRT matches where a never-seen char coincidentally matches the predicted tuple.");
  render_p(sec_rn, "CRT Restricted NN Theorem: restricting CRT nearest-neighbor to the 27 training-observed characters improves L0 from 37 to 40 (+8%, 3.33x over hard) and halves ghost matches from 6 to 3. Per-channel accuracy improves: 230 to 242 ppt (+5%), driven by strategy channels (ALIVE +16, DEAD +1). However, L6v and L_ctx are unchanged (45, 46) -- voting already subsumes the restriction. Ghost matches are NOT the primary bottleneck: only 6/43 score-7 were wrong. The bottleneck is prediction quality (229 ppt input). 14/14 WASI, 1340 params, zero added.");
  let grn = make_findings_grid(sec_rn) in
  render_finding(grn, "L0: 37->40 (+8%)", "333% over hard", "Restricting NN search from 95 to 27 observed characters improves L0 from 37 to 40. Modest but real. The mechanism: among score-7 candidates, restriction removes non-observed chars, so the NN selects the correct observed char instead of a ghost. Codebook shrinks 3.5x (95->27), accuracy up 8%. Zero additional parameters.", "S1597");
  render_finding(grn, "Ghosts halved: 6->3", "not eliminated", "Full NN: 43 score-7, 37 correct, 6 ghosts. Restricted NN: 43 score-7, 40 correct, 3 ghosts. Ghost matches halved but 3 remain -- these are observed chars that match the predicted tuple but are not the actual character. Score-7 count identical in both (43): restriction changes WHICH char is selected, not how many positions have perfect matches.", "S1597");
  render_finding(grn, "Per-ch ppt: 230->242", "ALIVE +16", "Restricted NN chooses from fewer candidates, yielding chars closer to actual on average. ALIVE channels gain +16 (191->207), DEAD only +1 (130->131). Strategy channels (K, L, GATE, ESCAPE) benefit disproportionately from restriction -- consistent with 490 split: strategy is more sensitive to codebook quality.", "S1597");
  render_finding(grn, "Voting subsumes", "L6v=45, L_ctx=46", "At L6v (joint voting) and L_ctx (contextual conditioning), restricted and unrestricted NN give identical results. The voting pipeline already disambiguates at positions where restriction would help. Restriction is a pure L0-stage optimization, subsumed by deeper pipeline layers. The bottleneck is per-channel prediction quality (229 ppt), not codebook size.", "S1597");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_rn)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bd() = (
  let sec_tc = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_tc, "9bd. Trigram Context (S1598)");
  render_p(sec_tc, "S1597 identified per-channel prediction quality (229 ppt) as the bottleneck. Bigrams use 1-step context: given prev residue, predict next. Trigrams use 2-step context: given prev-2 and prev-1 residues, predict next. q^2 entries per channel. Coverage feasible for small moduli (D:100%, K:100%), sparse for large (b:14%). Hybrid: trigram when covered, bigram fallback. Same text corpus (cyclic English + 30% noise, 1008 chars).");
  render_p(sec_tc, "CRT Trigram Context Theorem: two-step per-channel predictions improve ppt from 229 to 293 (+28%), with ALL 7 channels improving. D +26%, K +14%, E +45%, b +38%, L +25%, G +15%, ESC +39%. CRT NN reconstruction improves dramatically: L0 37 to 58 (+57%). However, voting pipeline REGRESSES: L6v drops from 27 to 24 (-11%). This is the decorrelation mechanism from S1589: improved per-channel predictions disagree with bigram-based joint predictions used in voting. NN bypasses this because it searches the codebook directly from the CRT tuple. 14/14 WASI, 5090 params (1340 base + 3750 trigram).");
  let gtc = make_findings_grid(sec_tc) in
  render_finding(gtc, "Per-ch ppt: 229->293", "+28% all 7 channels", "Trigram context improves every channel. Surprise: E (+45%) and b (+38%) gain MORE than D (+26%) despite sparser coverage. Even 14% b-coverage (339/2401 pairs) beats bigram by 38% because the covered pairs are high-frequency. The coverage-accuracy tradeoff favors trigrams across the board. 92% of predictions use trigram, only 8% fall back to bigram.", "S1598");
  render_finding(gtc, "NN L0: 37->58", "+57% (largest Phase A lift)", "Better per-channel predictions produce CRT tuples closer to valid characters. The 95-char NN codebook search benefits dramatically: 37 to 58 correct. This is the largest single-step improvement in Phase A. No added parameters -- the improvement is FREE from better predictions feeding into the same NN search.", "S1598");
  render_finding(gtc, "Voting drops: L6v 27->24", "decorrelation mechanism", "The voting pipeline mixes per-channel predictions with joint bigram predictions (DK, DL, etc.). When per-channel predictions improve via trigrams but joints stay bigram-based, they DISAGREE more. Voting averaging amplifies the disagreement. Same decorrelation mechanism as S1589 (2x data). Fix: trigram joint predictions (joint trigram tables), or NN-only pipeline.", "S1598");
  render_finding(gtc, "Coverage modulus-ordered", "D:100% K:100% b:14%", "Trigram coverage is modulus-determined: 806 training trigrams fill q^2 pairs. D (64 pairs) = 100%, K (81) = 100%, E (625) = 50%, b (2401) = 14%, L (121) = 92%, G (169) = 88%, ESC (289) = 66%. Coverage hierarchy follows the ring: small moduli have complete trigram data, large moduli are sparse. But even sparse coverage helps because high-frequency transitions are covered first.", "S1598");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_tc)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9be() = (
  let sec_tj = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_tj, "9be. Trigram Joint (S1599)");
  render_p(sec_tj, "S1598 improved per-channel predictions to 293 ppt but voting L6v REGRESSED from 27 to 24 due to decorrelation between trigram per-channel and bigram joint predictions. This test extends 3 joint tables (DK, DL, KL) from bigram to trigram context, aligning context depth across prediction sources. DG, KG, DE keep bigram (count buffers too large). Side-by-side comparison of method A (bigram joints) vs method B (trigram joints).");
  render_p(sec_tj, "CRT Trigram Joint Theorem: extending joint predictions to trigram context produces marginal voting recovery (L6v 24 to 25, +4%) but reveals CONDITIONAL DECORRELATION: L_ctx drops from 36 to 32 (-11%). CRT nearest-neighbor bypasses ALL decorrelation layers: B+NN L6v 45 to 62 (+38%), B+NN L_ctx 47 to 58 (+23%). Three-level decorrelation hierarchy discovered: (1) per-ch/joint at voting, (2) voting/conditional at conditioning, (3) compound. NN is structurally immune because it searches the CRT codebook directly. 14/14 WASI, 27819 params.");
  let gtj = make_findings_grid(sec_tj) in
  render_finding(gtj, "NN L6v: 45->62", "+38% (NN bypasses decorrelation)", "Method B voting feeds better-aligned D/K/L predictions to the CRT nearest-neighbor search. NN L6v jumps from 45 to 62. This is the largest single-step NN improvement in Phase A. The joint trigram alignment improves the CRT tuple that NN searches over, and NN converts that improvement directly into character accuracy without any cross-source averaging.", "S1599");
  render_finding(gtj, "Decorrelation hierarchy", "3 levels discovered", "Level 1: per-ch trigram vs joint bigram (S1598 voting regression). Level 2: voted trigram vs conditional bigram (S1599, L_ctx drops). Level 3: compound cross-layer. Each alignment fix at one level can expose decorrelation at the next. Voting and conditioning are inherently cross-source averagers. NN is the only mechanism that avoids all levels simultaneously.", "S1599");
  render_finding(gtj, "Joint trigram usage: 79%", "477/597 predictions", "Despite sparse coverage (DK 6%, DL 4%, KL 3% of joint pairs), 79% of test-time predictions use at least one joint trigram table. High-frequency joint pairs dominate: the training data visits a concentrated set of joint residue transitions. DK used 162/199, DL 158/199, KL 157/199 test positions.", "S1599");
  render_finding(gtj, "L_ctx: 36->32", "conditional decorrelation", "Conditional tables (b|D, ESC|D) are trained with bigram-depth data. When voted D improves via trigram alignment, the conditional lookup changes, creating a new context-depth mismatch. Hard L_ctx drops from 36 to 32. But NN L_ctx improves: 47 to 58. The conditional path has structural decorrelation that NN bypasses.", "S1599");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_tj)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bf() = (
  let sec_np = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_np, "9bf. NN Pipeline (S1600)");
  render_p(sec_np, "S1599 discovered a 3-level decorrelation hierarchy: voting, conditioning, and compound cross-layer, each introducing disagreement between prediction sources. NN bypassed all levels. This test strips voting and conditioning entirely, replacing them with multi-candidate NN selection. 5 candidate CRT tuples are generated from per-channel trigram + joint trigram predictions: per-ch only (C0), DK override (C1), DL override (C2), KL override (C3), and DK+KL union (C4). For each, NN searches 95 printable ASCII chars for the best channel match.");
  render_p(sec_np, "CRT NN Pipeline Theorem: joint trigram override with NN-only (no voting, no conditioning) outperforms both per-channel-only NN (58) and voting-based NN (62, S1599). DL override achieves 64/199 (+10% over per-ch, +3% over voting). All three joint overrides beat per-ch: DK=61, DL=64, KL=62. DL captures DEAD/ALIVE coupling (D=2 in DEAD, L=11 in ALIVE), the longest-range cross-channel correlation. Best-of-5 score selection (63) is suboptimal: NN score is not perfectly correlated with correctness. 14/14 WASI, 26611 params.");
  let gnp = make_findings_grid(sec_np) in
  render_finding(gnp, "DL override: 64", "+10% over per-ch, +3% over voting", "DL joint prediction overrides D (channel 0) and L (channel 4), capturing the DEAD/ALIVE coupling. D is in DEAD (inner, perception), L is in ALIVE (boundary, strategy). This is the longest-range cross-channel correlation among the three pairs. DK (channels 0,1) and KL (channels 1,4) touch closer channels. The axiom's 490 split predicts that DEAD/ALIVE coupling carries the most information.", "S1600");
  render_finding(gnp, "All overrides beat per-ch", "DK:61 DL:64 KL:62", "Every joint trigram override, used alone, beats per-channel-only NN (58). Joint predictions capture cross-channel correlations that independent per-channel predictions miss. The joint tables are sparse (3-6% coverage) but high-frequency pairs dominate test-time usage: DK 162/199, DL 158/199, KL 157/199.", "S1600");
  render_finding(gnp, "Best-of-5: 63 < DL: 64", "score != correctness", "Multi-candidate selection by NN score picks the candidate with the most channel matches. But this is suboptimal: fixed DL override is simpler and more accurate. NN score measures CRT tuple consistency, not prediction correctness. A candidate can have high score (many channels match some ASCII char) but that char can be wrong. Fixed DL override is the better pipeline.", "S1600");
  render_finding(gnp, "Winner dist: C0 40%", "per-ch wins by score, not accuracy", "Per-ch (C0) wins the NN score contest 79/199 times (40%), but only achieves 58/199 accuracy. DL (C2) wins only 32/199 (16%) by score, yet achieves 64/199 accuracy. Joint candidates collectively win 60% of score contests. Score-based selection overfavors per-ch because its CRT tuples are internally consistent but not necessarily correct.", "S1600");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_np)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bg() = (
  let sec_ao = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_ao, "9bg. Context Crossing (S1601)");
  render_p(sec_ao, "S1600 found DL override best (64/199) with trigram joints: DL > KL > DK. Is L special, or does any DEAD/ALIVE pair work? This test expands to 6 pairs with bigram joint overrides for a controlled comparison: DK, DL, KL (existing) + DG, DESC, DE (new). 4 cross pairs (DEAD+ALIVE) vs 2 same-half controls (KL=ALIVE+ALIVE, DE=DEAD+DEAD). All pairs use bigram for fairness.");
  render_p(sec_ao, "CRT Context Crossing Theorem: at bigram context, ALIVE+ALIVE pair (KL=63) dominates over all DEAD+ALIVE cross pairs (best DK=61). This REVERSES S1600's trigram result (DL=64 best). The 490 split advantage is context-dependent: ALIVE-pair dominance at bigram crosses to DEAD/ALIVE dominance at trigram. 4/6 overrides beat per-ch (58). DG(57) and DE(57) fall below -- they override high-accuracy channels (G=61ppt, E=64ppt), losing more from context reduction than gaining from correlation. KL succeeds because K(57ppt) and L(55ppt) have LOW per-ch accuracy -- less to lose. 14/14 WASI, 4581 params.");
  let gao = make_findings_grid(sec_ao) in
  render_finding(gao, "KL=63 best (bigram)", "reverses trigram DL=64", "At 1-step (bigram) context, ALIVE+ALIVE pair KL dominates. At 2-step (trigram, S1600), DEAD+ALIVE pair DL dominates. The pair ranking reverses between context depths. This means the 490 split advantage is not absolute -- it emerges only with sufficient temporal context. Cross-channel DEAD/ALIVE correlations are deeper structure that requires more context to exploit.", "S1601");
  render_finding(gao, "H1 FALSIFIED at bigram", "cross avg 59.25 < same avg 60.0", "The 490 split does NOT predict pair quality at bigram level. 4 cross pairs average 59.25/199. 2 same-half pairs average 60.0/199. ALIVE+ALIVE (KL=63) outperforms all cross pairs. Context depth is the moderating variable: insufficient context prevents cross-channel advantage from manifesting.", "S1601");
  render_finding(gao, "Override quality = inverse", "of per-ch accuracy", "DG and DE override high-accuracy channels (G:61, E:64 per-ch ppt) and fall BELOW per-ch NN (57 vs 58). KL overrides low-accuracy channels (K:57, L:55 per-ch ppt) and rises to 63. Bigram override helps when the replaced channels have weak trigram predictions -- less to lose. Override gain = correlation gain minus context loss.", "S1601");
  render_finding(gao, "Coverage: 26-27 distinct", "limited by 27-char alphabet", "All 6 bigram joint tables cover 26-27 distinct prev values out of 72-200 possible. This is capped by the 27 unique characters in the corpus: each char maps to exactly one joint residue pair. Override usage is 100% (199/199 for all pairs) because the 26-27 covered values capture the entire character distribution.", "S1601");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_ao)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bh() = (
  let sec_sc = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_sc, "9bh. Context Scaling (S1602)");
  render_p(sec_sc, "S1598 showed trigram per-channel predictions improved ppt by 28% over bigram. Does deeper context continue the trend? This test extends to 4-gram (3-position history) for 5 channels where q^3 is feasible: D(512), K(729), L(1331), G(2197), ESC(4913). E and b stay at trigram (q^3 too large). Three-level fallback: 4-gram if covered, trigram if covered, bigram otherwise.");
  render_p(sec_sc, "CRT Context Scaling Theorem: scaling CONTINUES but DECELERATES dramatically. bigram to trigram: +64 ppt (+28%). trigram to 4-gram: +15 ppt (+5.1%). A 4.3x deceleration. Trigram captures 81% of the total context improvement (64 of 79 ppt lift from 229 to 308). D drives the 4-gram lift: +22% (53 to 65), contributing 12 of 15 additional ppt. ESC REGRESSES at 10% coverage (-3%): sparse 4-gram keys are noisier than trigram modes. Coverage threshold: ~20% needed for 4-gram to help. NN L0 reaches 64, a new Phase A record. 14/14 WASI, 13564 params.");
  let gsc = make_findings_grid(sec_sc) in
  render_finding(gsc, "Scaling decelerates 4x", "trigram is near-optimal", "bigram to trigram: +64 ppt. trigram to 4-gram: +15 ppt. The 4.3x deceleration confirms diminishing returns from deeper per-channel context. Trigram captures 81% of total improvement. Going beyond 4-gram for most channels is unlikely to add significant accuracy. The context scaling law follows an exponential decay pattern.", "S1602");
  render_finding(gsc, "D drives 4-gram lift", "+22% (12 of 15 ppt gain)", "D-channel jumps from 53 to 65 at 4-gram (+22%), accounting for 12 of the 15 ppt total improvement. D is the coarsest channel (q=8, only 512 4-gram keys, 62% coverage). Coarser channels benefit most from deeper context because their predictions are more discriminable with additional history. Tower_D depth-3 manifests as context-depth sensitivity.", "S1602");
  render_finding(gsc, "ESC regresses at 10% cov", "sparse coverage = noise", "ESC drops from 57 to 55 at 4-gram (-3%). At 10% coverage (506/4913 keys), most 4-gram entries have only 1-2 observations. The mode from 1 observation is noise, not signal. Coverage threshold: G at 22% gains +4%, L at 31% gains +9%, ESC at 10% loses -3%. Approximately 20% coverage is needed for 4-gram to help.", "S1602");
  render_finding(gsc, "NN L0: 37->58->64", "new Phase A record", "NN nearest-neighbor search improves continuously: 37 (bigram) to 58 (trigram, +57%) to 64 (4-gram, +10%). Per-ch ppt improved only +5% (293 to 308) but NN gained +10%. NN amplifies small per-channel improvements. The 4-gram NN (64) matches the trigram DL override (64, S1600): deeper per-channel context substitutes for cross-channel correlation.", "S1602");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_sc)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bi() = (
  let sec_cs = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_cs, "9bi. Context-Correlation Substitution (S1603)");
  render_p(sec_cs, "S1602 showed 4-gram per-channel NN=64. S1600 showed trigram DL override NN=64. Same number, different paths. Are context depth and cross-channel correlation additive (compound) or substitutive (same information)? This test combines 4-gram per-channel predictions with joint trigram overrides: 5 candidates compared. C0: 4-gram per-ch only. C1: 4-gram + DL override. C2: 4-gram + DK override. C3: 4-gram + KL override. C4: trigram + DL override (replicates S1600).");
  render_p(sec_cs, "CRT Context-Correlation Substitution Theorem: per-channel context depth (4-gram) and cross-channel joint correlation (DL/KL trigram override) extract the SAME predictive information from CRT-decomposed text. Four independent paths -- 4-gram per-ch, 4-gram+DL, 4-gram+KL, trigram+DL -- all achieve NN 64/199. The only pair that drops is DK (63). High divergence (more than 50% of positions differ between candidates) proves the paths use genuinely different predictions yet hit the same ceiling. Hard CRT diverges sharply (18 vs 26) because CRT reconstruction is sensitive to correlation structure that NN's soft decoding equalizes. The ceiling is a property of the data (corpus size and complexity), not the model. 14/14 WASI, 36293 params.");
  let gcs = make_findings_grid(sec_cs) in
  render_finding(gcs, "All paths converge to 64", "substitution confirmed", "C0 (4gram per-ch) = 64. C1 (4gram+DL) = 64. C3 (4gram+KL) = 64. C4 (tri+DL) = 64. C2 (4gram+DK) = 63. Four of five candidates hit exactly 64/199. Context depth and cross-channel correlation are two views of the SAME underlying transition structure in the text. Neither adds information the other lacks. This is a D=2 pair: two complementary perspectives on one phenomenon.", "S1603");
  render_finding(gcs, "Hard CRT diverges: 18 vs 26", "NN equalizes, CRT does not", "Hard CRT L0 at 4-gram per-ch: 18/199. Hard CRT L0 at trigram+DL: 26/199. DL override improves hard CRT reconstruction by 44%. But NN shows no difference (both 64). CRT reconstruction requires all 7 channels to be exactly correct simultaneously. Joint predictions improve channel coherence (inter-channel consistency), which helps hard CRT. NN's soft nearest-neighbor decoding tolerates channel errors, equalizing the two paths.", "S1603");
  render_finding(gcs, "Divergence over 50%", "different predictions, same accuracy", "C1 differs from C0 on 114/199 positions (57%). C2 on 124 (62%). C3 on 109 (55%). The joint overrides change more than HALF the per-position CRT tuples. These are not minor perturbations but fundamentally different prediction strategies. Yet accuracy is identical. The information content is the same; only the encoding differs.", "S1603");
  render_finding(gcs, "DK sole drop: 63", "weakest pair at deeper context", "DK override is the only candidate below 64. At bigram (S1601): DK=61 (but DL=59, so DK beats DL there). At trigram (S1600): DK=61 (DL=64, KL=62). At 4-gram base: DK=63. DK is never the best pair at any context level. D and K are close neighbors on the chain (channels 0 and 1). Their correlation is shorter-range than DL (D=DEAD, L=ALIVE, longest span) or KL (both ALIVE but distant).", "S1603");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_cs)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bj() = (
  let sec_ds = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_ds, "9bj. Data Scaling (S1604)");
  render_p(sec_ds, "S1603 proved context and correlation are substitutes: the 64/199 ceiling is DATA-LIMITED. Direct test: clean A/B comparison on the SAME test set. Condition A: train on 808 chars. Condition B: train on 1616 chars (2x). Both tested on positions 1616-2015 (399 predictions). Corpus: 2016-char text from a 252-char English seed (two 126-char phrases for transition diversity, adding j/k/w/x to the vocabulary). Pipeline: trigram per-ch + DL joint trigram override + NN.");
  render_p(sec_ds, "CRT Data Scaling Theorem: doubling training data on the same corpus raises the NN ceiling from 73 to 93 per-ch (+27%) and from 81 to 103 with DL override (+27%). Per-channel accuracy improves 220 to 247 ppt (+12%). DL override lifts NN by 8 (A) and 10 (B) -- correlation is NOT a substitute at this diversity level, contradicting S1603's finding on the less diverse corpus. Hard CRT REVERSES: per-ch drops 10 to 7 despite better per-channel accuracy (decorrelation mechanism, S1589). Hard DL is stable (21 to 20). b coverage grows sub-linearly: 14% to 20% (+38%, not 2x). 14/14 WASI, 11626 params.");
  let gds = make_findings_grid(sec_ds) in
  render_finding(gds, "NN ceiling moves: 73 to 93", "+27% from 2x data", "NN per-ch accuracy: 73/399 (A, 808 train) to 93/399 (B, 1616 train) on the same 399-position test set. The ceiling IS data-limited as S1603 predicted, and it DOES move with more data. NN DL: 81 to 103 (+27%). Both pipelines benefit equally from data scaling. The 252-char seed provides enough transition diversity for the additional data to matter.", "S1604");
  render_finding(gds, "DL adds value: not a substitute", "DL lift 8 (A) and 10 (B)", "DL override adds 8 NN-correct predictions in Condition A and 10 in Condition B. S1603 found DL = per-ch (both 64) with a 126-char seed corpus. With the more diverse 252-char seed, DL consistently helps. The substitution theorem is CORPUS-DEPENDENT: in low-diversity text where per-ch captures most transitions, correlation adds nothing. With more transition diversity, joint DL captures patterns that per-ch trigrams miss.", "S1604");
  render_finding(gds, "Hard CRT reverses: 10 to 7", "decorrelation amplified by data", "Hard CRT per-ch DECREASES from 10 (A) to 7 (B) despite better per-channel predictions (220 to 247 ppt). This is the S1589 decorrelation mechanism: improved per-channel predictions become more diverse across channels, reducing the probability that all 7 channels are simultaneously correct. Hard DL is stable (21 to 20) because joint DL predictions maintain inter-channel coherence. NN bypasses decorrelation entirely: 73 to 93 per-ch improvement.", "S1604");
  render_finding(gds, "Coverage sub-linear: b 14% to 20%", "diversity limits large-modulus channels", "b trigram coverage: 356 to 493 out of 2401 (14% to 20%, +38%). E coverage: 329 to 444 (52% to 71%). D and K already at 100%. ESC: 69% to 85%. 2x data improves coverage everywhere but sub-linearly for large-modulus channels. The 252-char seed uses 27 unique chars whose mod-49 residues cluster. Corpus diversity, not size, remains the binding constraint for b.", "S1604");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_ds)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bk() = (
  let sec_bt = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_bt, "9bk. Bloom Text (S1605)");
  render_p(sec_bt, "S1589 proved: per-channel predictions decorrelate with more data -- pairwise accuracy rises but 7-way CRT reconstruction FALLS. S1604 confirmed decorrelation amplifies with 2x data (hard CRT 10 to 7). Root cause: channels become independently accurate on DIFFERENT transitions, so P(all 7 correct) drops. Bloom mixing is the principled fix: use cross-channel joint predictions to force per-channel consensus. 6 joint bigram pairs (DK, DL, KL, DG, DESC, DE), each storing the most common FULL next character. For each channel: majority vote across per-channel trigram + 6 full-character pair extractions.");
  render_p(sec_bt, "CRT Bloom Text Theorem: bloom voting triples hard CRT reconstruction (15 to 46 out of 199, +207%) while reducing NN accuracy (58 to 46, -21%). Bloom is a COHERENCE mechanism: it forces per-channel predictions toward consistent full-character tuples, making NN score approximately equal hard CRT (46 and 46). Weighting per-channel trigram (w=1,3,5) does not change the result because 6 correlated joint votes always dominate. Oracle (best-of per-ch or bloom) reaches 70/199, showing the two approaches capture COMPLEMENTARY information. 14/14 WASI, 4581 params.");
  let gbt = make_findings_grid(sec_bt) in
  render_finding(gbt, "Hard CRT: 15 to 46", "+207%, 3.07x", "Per-channel trigram produces 15/199 exact CRT reconstructions. Bloom voting produces 46/199 -- a 3.07x improvement. Best single-pair override (DL) gives only 17 hard CRT. Bloom outperforms the best individual override by 2.7x. The mechanism: 6 joint pairs predict correlated full characters, and per-channel majority voting forces all 7 channels toward the consensus character. When that character is correct, all channels match simultaneously.", "S1605");
  render_finding(gbt, "NN: 58 to 46", "coherence-accuracy trade-off", "NN accuracy drops from 58 to 46 with bloom voting. NN is forgiving -- it finds the nearest match even with imperfect channels. Bloom degrades E (64 to 48) and b (62 to 46), the large-modulus channels where joint pairs have weakest coverage. But bloom improves D (53 to 59). The trade-off: bloom sacrifices per-channel accuracy for cross-channel coherence, which helps hard CRT (all-or-nothing) but hurts NN (partial matches count).", "S1605");
  render_finding(gbt, "Oracle: 70/199", "complementary information", "If an oracle could pick the correct answer from per-ch or bloom predictions, it would achieve 70/199 (35.2%). Per-ch=58, bloom=46, overlap=34. So 24 positions are uniquely correct for per-ch and 12 are uniquely correct for bloom. The 12 bloom-only positions represent transitions where cross-channel coherence reveals the correct answer that independent channels miss. Combining both approaches optimally could yield a 21% improvement over per-ch alone.", "S1605");
  render_finding(gbt, "Weight invariant", "w=1,3,5 all give 46", "Weighting the per-channel trigram vote (w=1: equal, w=3: triple, w=5: heavy) produces identical NN and hard CRT results. The 6 joint pair predictions are strongly correlated (all conditioned on aspects of the same previous character), so they form a bloc that outvotes the per-channel prediction at any tested weight. Only w>=7 would give per-channel enough votes to break the bloc. The correlation of joint predictions is itself a structural property: CRT decomposition of the same character produces correlated residues.", "S1605");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_bt)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bl() = (
  let sec_sb = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_sb, "9bl. Strict Bloom (S1606)");
  render_p(sec_sb, "S1605 showed bloom voting triples hard CRT but costs 21% NN accuracy. Root cause hypothesis: each joint pair votes on ALL 7 channels, but only 2 are meaningful. DK knows nothing about b -- its b-channel vote is noise extracted from a full-character prediction. Strict bloom: each pair votes only on its own 2 channels. Coverage: D has 5 pairs (strong consensus), K and L have 2 each (moderate), E/G/ESC have 1 each (light), b has NONE (pure per-channel, protected).");
  render_p(sec_sb, "CRT Strict Bloom Theorem: restricting bloom votes to pair-owned channels protects b-channel accuracy (62 vs 46, +35% recovery) but FAILS to improve NN (45 vs 46) and loses most hard CRT gain (20 vs 46). The coherence mechanism requires ALL-channel consensus -- partial consensus is insufficient. Full bloom's power comes from forcing all 7 channels toward the SAME character, creating correlated noise that aligns channels. Removing irrelevant votes removes the correlation. The coherence-accuracy trade-off is structural. 14/14 WASI, 4581 params.");
  let gsb = make_findings_grid(sec_sb) in
  render_finding(gsb, "b-channel: 62 vs 46", "structural protection", "b has zero pair coverage in strict bloom, so it keeps its per-channel trigram prediction. Per-ch b=62, full bloom b=46 (-26%), strict b=62 (0% loss). This is the ONLY channel where strict bloom unambiguously helps. The b-channel is the deepest data channel (mod 49) with the most information per residue -- protecting it preserves the most per-channel accuracy.", "S1606");
  render_finding(gsb, "NN: 45 vs 46 vs 58", "strict WORSE than full", "Strict bloom NN (45) is slightly WORSE than full bloom (46), not better. Both far below per-ch (58). Protecting b (+16 correct residues) is offset by G (-6) and ESC (-5) degradation. G and ESC each have only 1 pair coverage under strict -- the single pair prediction is worse than the smoothed 6-vote average in full bloom. Correlated noise across all pairs helps more than restricting to relevant pairs.", "S1606");
  render_finding(gsb, "Hard CRT: 20 vs 46 vs 15", "partial consensus insufficient", "Strict hard CRT (20) is modestly above per-ch (15) but far below full bloom (46). Hard CRT requires ALL 7 channels correct simultaneously. Under strict, only D gets strong consensus (5 pairs). b stays decorrelated. Without all-channel agreement, P(all 7 correct) remains low. Full bloom forces agreement by making all channels vote on the same character, which is exactly what hard CRT needs.", "S1606");
  render_finding(gsb, "Divergence: 572 vs 867", "strict touches fewer channels", "Strict bloom changes 572 channel predictions vs 867 for full bloom (34% fewer). This is expected: strict bloom only modifies channels with pair coverage. b is never changed (0 pairs). But LESS divergence means LESS coherence, and less coherence means less hard CRT gain. The divergence count IS the coherence mechanism.", "S1606");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_sb)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bm() = (
  let sec_as = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_as, "9bm. Bloom-Agreement Selection (S1607)");
  render_p(sec_as, "S1605 proved bloom and per-ch capture COMPLEMENTARY information: oracle 70/199 vs per-ch 58 and bloom 46. The question: can we SELECT between them WITHOUT the oracle? Candidate signal: bloom-agreement count -- how many of 7 channels match between bloom and per-ch predictions. When bloom changes many channels, it has made a strong (possibly wrong) commitment. When bloom agrees, the joint pairs are consistent with per-channel predictions.");
  render_p(sec_as, "CRT Bloom-Agreement Selection Theorem: agreement count partitions test positions into 8 buckets with dramatically different accuracy profiles. COUNTER-INTUITIVE: when bloom strongly disagrees (0-1 channels matching), trust PER-CH, not bloom. Bloom's confident disagreement means the joint pairs converged on a coherent-but-wrong character. The reversed threshold (per-ch when agree<2, bloom when agree>=2) achieves NN 64/199 -- closing 50% of the gap from per-ch (58) to oracle (70) with ZERO additional parameters. Hard CRT: 47/199 (preserving bloom's 3x advantage). 14/14 WASI, 4581 params.");
  let gas = make_findings_grid(sec_as) in
  render_finding(gas, "Selection NN: 64/199", "+10% over per-ch, gap to oracle 6", "Best rule: trust per-ch when agreement < 2 (80 positions, 40%), trust bloom when agreement >= 2 (119 positions, 60%). Per-ch contributes 22/80 correct in low-agreement (28%), bloom contributes 42/119 in high-agreement (35%). The 80 low-agreement positions are where bloom changes 6-7 channels -- a strong prediction that is wrong 95% of the time for bloom but 28% correct for per-ch. Zero additional parameters: the selection signal is derived from the model itself.", "S1607");
  render_finding(gas, "Counter-intuitive direction", "high divergence = per-ch wins", "Naive expectation: when bloom strongly disagrees, bloom has unique information. Reality: agree=0 (31 positions) has per-ch 11 correct, bloom 3. Agree=1 (49 positions) has per-ch 11, bloom 1. The mechanism: 6 joint pairs agree on a WRONG full character, forcing all channels to match. All-wrong coherence (coherent-but-wrong) is worse than independent partial correctness (some channels right, NN exploits them).", "S1607");
  render_finding(gas, "Hard CRT: 47 vs 46 vs 15", "best of both worlds", "Selection preserves bloom's hard CRT advantage (47 vs bloom 46, per-ch 15) because the >= 2 agreement positions include most hard CRT successes. The 16 full-agreement (7/7) positions contribute 10 hard CRT hits. Medium agreement (2-6) contributes 32. Low agreement (<2) contributes 5 via per-ch. Hard CRT success concentrates in moderate-to-high agreement, exactly where the selection trusts bloom.", "S1607");
  render_finding(gas, "8 populated buckets", "non-trivial distribution", "All 8 agreement levels (0-7) have positions. Agree=1 is largest (49 positions, 25%). Agree=7 smallest populated (16, 8%). 183/199 positions have bloom diverging from per-ch on at least 1 channel. The distribution is left-skewed: bloom changes more channels than it keeps (median agreement ~2). The heavy left tail (80 positions at agree<2) provides the selection leverage.", "S1607");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_as)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bn() = (
  let sec_dd = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_dd, "9bn. Distribution Decoding (S1608)");
  render_p(sec_dd, "The CRT nearest-neighbor decoder (S1595) uses binary matching: for each candidate character, score = number of channels where c mod q equals the predicted residue. This discards confidence -- a channel with 99% support on its prediction contributes the same +1 as one with 30% support. And a candidate matching the 2nd-best residue in 3 channels gets 0. Three decoders compared: C0 binary NN (baseline), C1 confidence-weighted NN (weight matches by best_count/total), C2 full distribution (Bayesian scoring with Laplace smoothing over all residue counts).");
  render_p(sec_dd, "CRT Distribution Decoding Theorem: confidence-weighted CRT decoding improves binary NN by 5.2% (58 to 61 out of 199) with zero additional parameters. Full distribution decode also improves (+3.4%, 58 to 60) but less than confidence weighting. The argmax IS the right prediction -- its confidence is the missing metadata. Oracle of binary + distribution reaches 68/199, showing complementary errors. Binary finds 8 chars distribution misses; distribution finds 10 chars binary misses. 14/14 WASI, 3882 base params (improvement is free).");
  let gdd = make_findings_grid(sec_dd) in
  render_finding(gdd, "C1 conf-weighted: 61", "+5.2%, zero additional params", "Confidence-weighted NN scores each channel match by best_count*100/(total+1). High-confidence channels contribute more to the NN score. For dense channels (D at 100% trigram coverage), confidence ranges 20-80, adding discrimination. For sparse channels with bigram fallback, default confidence=50. This is the sweet spot between information loss (binary: ignores confidence) and noise (full distribution: considers all residues). The improvement is FREE: confidence is derived from existing count data.", "S1608");
  render_finding(gdd, "C2 distribution: 60", "Bayesian + Laplace", "Full distribution scoring: for each candidate c, sum (count[context, c mod q] + 1) * 1000 / (total + q) across 7 channels. Laplace smoothing ensures every candidate gets nonzero score from every channel. Sparse channels (b at 14% trigram coverage) give near-uniform scores (1000/49 = 20 per candidate), effectively zero signal. This honest uncertainty avoids the noisy bigram fallback that binary NN uses. But considering all residues also considers noise, slightly hurting relative to confidence-only.", "S1608");
  render_finding(gdd, "Oracle(C0|C2): 68", "complementary errors", "Binary NN and distribution decode make different errors. 132/199 positions have the same prediction. Of the 67 differing positions: 8 are C0-only correct, 10 are C2-only correct, 50 are both correct (from the 132 agreeing). The 10 C2-only positions: distribution finds characters where the correct residue was 2nd-best in several channels (binary scores 0 for these, distribution scores proportional to count). The 8 C0-only positions: binary benefits from its bigram fallback on sparse channels where C2 gives uniform.", "S1608");
  render_finding(gdd, "Confidence > distribution", "argmax is the right choice", "C1 (confidence-weighted, using argmax) beats C2 (full distribution) by 1 character. The argmax prediction is almost always correct when it has high confidence. The full distribution diffuses credit across multiple residues, which helps in ambiguous cases but adds noise in clear cases. The optimal strategy: use the argmax prediction but weight it by confidence. This captures the key metadata (how sure is this channel?) without the noise of considering non-argmax residues.", "S1608");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_dd)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bo() = (
  let sec_cs = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_cs, "9bo. Confidence-Selection Composition (S1609)");
  render_p(sec_cs, "S1607 improved the source (selection: per-ch vs bloom based on agreement, 58 to 64). S1608 improved the decoder (confidence weighting, 58 to 61). These two improvements address different parts of the pipeline: selection chooses WHICH 7-channel predictions to use, while C1 chooses HOW to decode those predictions into a character. The composition question: do they stack?");
  render_p(sec_cs, "CRT Confidence-Selection Composition Theorem: the composition is SUB-ADDITIVE. C1 + selection yields 65/199 -- only +1 over C0 selection (64), not the expected +3 to +6. Root cause: C1 has ZERO effect on bloom predictions. Bloom and per-ch confidence-weighted NN produce identical results (46/199) at every agreement level. C1 only helps in the per-ch path. Under selection at T=2, only 80 of 199 positions use per-ch (agree<2). Of C1's +3 per-ch improvement, just +1 falls in these 80 positions -- the other +2 are wasted where bloom is selected instead. Oracle across all 4 methods: 72/199. 14/14 WASI, 4581 params.");
  let gcs = make_findings_grid(sec_cs) in
  render_finding(gcs, "C1 bloom = C0 bloom = 46", "zero effect", "Bloom voting forces cross-channel consensus by majority of 6 joint pairs + 1 per-ch vote. Per-ch confidence measures how sure the independent trigram model is about the per-ch prediction. When bloom overrides a channel, that confidence is irrelevant to the bloom prediction quality. Weighting bloom predictions by per-ch confidence does not change which candidates score highest -- the bloom consensus dominates regardless of weights. This is structural: consensus kills the variance that confidence weighting exploits.", "S1609");
  render_finding(gcs, "Sub-additive: 65 not 67", "overlap in per-ch path", "C1 improves per-ch from 58 to 61 (+3). Selection routes agree<2 positions to per-ch. Most of C1's +3 improvement falls within these positions. Of the 80 low-agreement positions: C0 gets 22 correct, C1 gets 23 (+1). The other +2 from C1 falls in agree>=2 positions where selection routes to bloom (where C1 has no effect). The overlap is structural: selection and C1 both help the same easy-to-predict positions.", "S1609");
  render_finding(gcs, "Best overall: 65/199", "+12% over C0 per-ch baseline", "The composition stack: C0 per-ch 58, C1 per-ch 61 (+5.2%), C0 selection 64 (+10.3%), C1 selection 65 (+12.1%). Each improvement is diminishing because the improvements partially overlap. The oracle (72) suggests 7 more positions are recoverable with a better composition strategy. The bloom path (46) has room to improve but needs bloom-specific confidence, not per-ch confidence.", "S1609");
  render_finding(gcs, "Orthogonality confirmed", "same T=2 threshold", "C0 and C1 find the same optimal agreement threshold (T=2). The agreement signal is independent of the decoder: it measures prediction-source quality (per-ch vs bloom), not decoder quality (binary vs confidence). This confirms that selection and C1 operate on orthogonal axes of the pipeline. Their sub-additivity comes from spatial overlap (same positions benefit from both), not from interference.", "S1609");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_cs)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bp() = (
  let sec_ds = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_ds, "9bp. Data Selection (S1610)");
  render_p(sec_ds, "S1604 showed 2x training data lifts per-channel NN from 73 to 93 (+27%). S1607-S1609 developed bloom-agreement selection (+10%) and confidence-weighted decoding (+4%). The composition question: do these decoder improvements scale with data, or does more data make them redundant?");
  render_p(sec_ds, "CRT Data Selection Theorem: bloom-agreement selection retains positive lift at 2x training data but the relative improvement diminishes from +20% (1x: 73 to 88) to +10% (2x: 93 to 102). Bloom NN is data-resistant (68 to 70, +3%) while per-channel NN scales strongly (73 to 93, +27%). The full stack attribution is roughly additive: data +20 (69%), confidence +4 (14%), selection +5 (17%). Data is king -- decoder improvements are free positive lift but account for only 31% of the total improvement. 14/14 WASI, 4581 params.");
  let gds = make_findings_grid(sec_ds) in
  render_finding(gds, "Selection still helps at 2x", "B sel=102 vs B pch=93", "At 2x data, the best selection (C1+agreement) achieves 102/399 (25.6%) vs per-channel-only 93/399 (23.3%). The +9 lift is real but diminished from +15 at 1x data. As per-channel quality improves, fewer positions benefit from bloom's coherent alternative. The marginal value of selection is inversely proportional to per-channel accuracy.", "S1610");
  render_finding(gds, "Bloom is data-resistant", "68 to 70 (+3%)", "Bloom NN barely moves with 2x data (68 to 70) while per-channel scales +27% (73 to 93). Bloom's full-character bigram tables have limited coverage: DK=72 entries, DE=200. Even with 2x data, many joint bigrams remain sparse. Per-channel trigrams benefit much more because individual channel transitions are much more likely to be covered. At very high data levels, per-ch will dominate and selection converges toward per-ch-only.", "S1610");
  render_finding(gds, "Hard CRT: bloom 70 vs per-ch 7", "decorrelation amplified", "Hard CRT per-channel DECREASES with more data (10 to 7, -30%) even though per-channel accuracy improves (220 to 247 ppt). More data makes per-channel predictions more accurate but also more decorrelated across channels (S1589 mechanism amplified). Bloom hard CRT stays stable (68 to 70) because consensus forces coherence. The decorrelation tax on hard CRT grows with data quality.", "S1610");
  render_finding(gds, "Full stack: data is king", "+29 from 73 to 102", "Attribution: data accounts for +20 of +29 total improvement (69%), confidence +4 (14%), selection +5 (17%). The three improvements are roughly additive with no interaction. Decoder improvements (C1 + selection) should always be applied -- they are free positive lift with zero additional parameters. But the bottleneck is prediction quality, which scales with data.", "S1610");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_ds)
)

fn page_ai_sec_9bq() = (
  let sec_lb = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_lb, "9bq. Learned Bloom (S1611)");
  render_p(sec_lb, "S1610 closed the decoder path. Bloom is data-resistant: majority vote (equal weight to all 6 joint pair sources) plateaus with more data. Hypothesis: per-source per-channel quality weights improve bloom by directing consensus toward the most reliable information sources for each channel. Method: split training data into learn-set (build tables) and validation-set (evaluate per-source per-channel accuracy to learn weights). 49 learned weights (7 sources x 7 channels).");
  render_p(sec_lb, "CRT Learned Bloom Theorem (FALSIFIED): per-source per-channel quality weights have zero effect on bloom voting when sources predict full characters. Learned bloom NN = equal bloom NN = 70/399. Zero delta. Root cause: the 6 joint pairs produce nearly identical per-channel weights because they predict FULL characters whose residues are deterministic. Sources 2-6 have D-weight 104-105, b-weight 70-71. Majority vote on correlated sources is already optimal (Condorcet). The bloom bottleneck is source DIVERSITY, not source WEIGHTING. 14/14 WASI, 4630 params.");
  let glb = make_findings_grid(sec_lb) in
  render_finding(glb, "Learned = Equal = 70", "ZERO EFFECT", "Learned-weight bloom NN = 70/399. Equal-weight bloom NN = 70/399. Identical result across C0, C1, hard CRT, and selection. The 49 learned weights (7 sources x 7 channels) add zero information because the per-channel residues extracted from full-character predictions are deterministic functions of the same underlying predictions.", "S1611");
  render_finding(glb, "Source correlation", "weights 68..105", "Weight matrix range is 68 (b from DK) to 105 (D from per-ch). But sources 2-6 (DL, KL, DG, DESC, DE) are nearly identical within each channel. Full-char predictions from overlapping pair bases are correlated: DK and DL share the D conditioning. When sources agree (which they mostly do), weighting is irrelevant. When they disagree, the disagreement pattern is not aligned with the quality-weight structure.", "S1611");
  render_finding(glb, "DK proximity structure", "D=99, b=68", "The DK pair predicts D-channel (99 correct) better than b-channel (68 correct) on 403 val positions. This confirms channel proximity (pairs predict their own channels better) but this structural signal cannot help bloom because the predictions are already full characters. Proximity structure would help if sources predicted per-channel residues independently.", "S1611");
  render_finding(glb, "Bloom bottleneck = diversity", "not weighting", "Equal-weight majority vote is optimal for correlated sources. To improve bloom, the sources themselves must be diversified: different conditioning bases, different prediction granularity, or different information sources entirely. The decoder path (S1607-S1610) and the weighting path (S1611) are both closed. Future improvement requires either more data or structurally different predictors.", "S1611");
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_lb)
)

/* === SEC 9br: HYPERRING CONTEXT (S1613 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9br() = (
  let sec_hr = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_hr, "9br. Hyperring Context (S1613)");
  render_p(sec_hr, "Probe 6: does the hyperring confidence gcd(char, N_TRANS) predict accuracy?");
  let ghr = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(ghr);
  render_p(ghr, "S1612 proved: hyperring division |a/a| = gcd(a, N) classifies each ring element by algebraic regularity. Units (gcd=1) have clean CRT fingerprints. Zero divisors (gcd>1) are algebraically ambiguous -- some channels carry no information. Test: split prediction accuracy by context character class.");
  render_finding(ghr, "Census: 19/95 units", "20% of printable ASCII", "Characters coprime to all 7 axiom primes {2,3,5,7,11,13,17} form the unit group. Common units: 'a'=97, 'e'=101, 'g'=103, 'k'=107, 'm'=109. Common ZDs: space=32 (gcd=8), 't'=116 (gcd=4), 'h'=104 (gcd=104=D^3*GATE), 'i'=105 (gcd=105=HYDOR=K*E*b). The character 'i' is maximally degenerate: zero residue in K, E, and b channels simultaneously.", "S1613");
  render_finding(ghr, "Unit context: 311 ppt", "vs ZD: 227 ppt (+37%)", "Prediction accuracy is 37% higher when the context (previous) character is a unit. This is NOT a corpus-frequency effect -- it reflects algebraic information content. A unit has independent non-zero residues in all 7 channels. A zero divisor has some channels mapping to zero, creating degenerate bigram conditioning.", "S1613");
  render_finding(ghr, "Channel-size dependence", "D:+146, K:+85, E:-54", "The unit-vs-ZD effect is CHANNEL-SIZE DEPENDENT. Small moduli (D=8: +146 ppt, K=9: +85, L=11: +94) see zero-residue context as degenerate: 12 diverse characters share residue-0 in D-channel. Large moduli (E=25: -54, ESCAPE=17: -49) see zero-residue as specific: only 4 chars share residue-0 in E-channel. The crossover is around modulus 11-13.", "S1613");
  render_finding(ghr, "Naive masking: NEUTRAL", "92 vs 93 baseline", "Channel masking (ignore channels where context residue = 0) is neutral overall. It helps for D and K (removing degenerate predictions) but hurts for E and ESCAPE (removing informative predictions from small specific groups). The non-uniform effect across channels means uniform masking cancels itself.", "S1613");
  render_finding(ghr, "Statistical subsumes algebraic", "C1=combined=99", "Combining algebraic masking with statistical confidence (C1) yields no improvement over C1 alone. The trigram confidence score already captures the information quality that the hyperring classification measures. The algebraic signal is REAL but redundant given statistical confidence.", "S1613");
  render_finding(ghr, "Hyperring confirmed", "observable but not exploitable via uniform mask", "The probe CONFIRMS the hyperring structure is computationally observable: unit contexts genuinely produce better predictions. But FALSIFIES the simple masking approach: the correct exploitation requires per-channel-size-dependent masking (mask small channels, keep large channels), which statistical confidence already approximates. 14/14 test_hyperring_attn.ax.", "S1613");
  dom_append(sec_hr, ghr);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_hr)
)

/* === SEC 9bs: CROSSMODAL BLOOM IMMUNITY (S1614 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9bs() = (
  let sec_cm = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_cm, "9bs. Crossmodal Bloom Immunity (S1614)");
  render_p(sec_cm, "Can adding a structurally different source break the bloom voting plateau? Test: vertical canvas bigram (stride 14 = D*b) as 8th bloom source alongside 1 per-channel trigram + 6 joint text pairs.");
  let gcm = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gcm);
  render_p(gcm, "S1611 proved equal-weight bloom is optimal for correlated sources. S1569 showed 54% table divergence between horizontal and vertical bigrams -- genuine structural diversity. Canvas: 14 columns x 72 rows, matching S1567 crossmodal layout. Vertical bigram: given text[p-13] (character directly above on 14-col canvas), predict text[p+1]. 794 training transitions, 27 unique context characters covered.");
  render_finding(gcm, "ZERO divergent positions", "0/199", "Adding 1 vertical full-character source to 7-source bloom changes the majority vote at ZERO positions across all 7 channels. The text-only and crossmodal bloom produce identical per-channel residues, identical NN predictions (both 46/199), and identical hard CRT (both 46/199). The 8th source is completely absorbed.", "S1614");
  render_finding(gcm, "87% correlated majority", "175/199 positions", "At 87% of test positions, 5 or more of the 6 joint text pairs predict the same full character. This creates a 5-vote majority in every CRT channel. Adding 1 additional source (whether correlated or uncorrelated) cannot overturn a 5-vote majority with 7-source baseline (maximum 2 dissenting votes vs 5 agreeing). This is the STRUCTURAL root cause of bloom immunity.", "S1614");
  render_finding(gcm, "Vertical IS diverse", "74% D-channel disagreement", "The vertical source disagrees with text-bloom D-channel prediction at 148/199 positions (74%). It provides genuinely different transition statistics. The problem is not lack of diversity -- it is that a single diverse source is outvoted by the correlated majority. Diversity is necessary but not sufficient: QUANTITY of diverse sources matters.", "S1614");
  render_finding(gcm, "Vertical standalone: 110 ppt", "22/199 full-char match", "The vertical bigram achieves 110 ppt standalone accuracy (1.1x random). Modest but real: spatial transitions contain predictive signal. For comparison, per-channel trigrams achieve 293 ppt. The spatial signal is weaker than sequential but structurally independent.", "S1614");
  render_finding(gcm, "Five paths now CLOSED", "decoder+weighting+strict+hyperring+crossmodal", "Decoder engineering (S1607-S1610), learned weighting (S1611), strict bloom (S1606), hyperring masking (S1613), and single-source crossmodal addition (S1614) are all closed. The bloom plateau is immune to single-source modifications. Breaking it requires either replacing correlated sources with per-channel-native predictors, or providing enough uncorrelated sources to outvote the correlated majority. 14/14 test_crossmodal_select.ax.", "S1614");
  dom_append(sec_cm, gcm);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_cm)
)

/* === SEC 9bt: PER-CHANNEL-NATIVE BLOOM (S1615 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9bt() = (
  let sec_nb = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_nb, "9bt. Per-Channel-Native Bloom (S1615)");
  render_p(sec_nb, "S1614 proved single-source addition is immune. The root cause: 6 full-character joint pairs predict the SAME character, creating correlated residues in ALL channels. Can we break this correlation by predicting per-channel residues DIRECTLY from pair conditioning?");
  let gnb = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gnb);
  render_p(gnb, "Per-channel-native: for each pair (DK, DL, KL, DG, DESC, DE) and each target channel, build a separate prediction table: joint_residue(prev) -> argmax(target_ch_residue(next)). 6 pairs x 7 channels = 42 tables. Each channel gets 6 independent native predictions plus 1 trigram = 7 sources. Native sources predict residues from DIFFERENT conditioning (DK uses K-context, DL uses L-context for D-channel), so they decorrelate by construction.");
  render_finding(gnb, "59% divergence", "118/199 positions", "Per-channel-native bloom diverges from full-char bloom at 118 of 199 test positions (59%). The sources ARE decorrelated: native bloom produces different per-channel residues than full-char bloom. D-channel: 81/199 divergent (highest). E-channel: 0/199 (zero divergence -- small modulus dominated by few residues). Decorrelation is real and measurable.", "S1615");
  render_finding(gnb, "NN: native 45 = FC 46", "within noise", "Despite 59% bloom divergence, NN soft decoding accuracy is essentially identical: native 45/199 vs full-char 46/199. Decorrelation changes the bloom predictions but does not improve the character those predictions reconstruct to. The NN ceiling is a property of the DATA, not of which bloom method generates the CRT tuple.", "S1615");
  render_finding(gnb, "Hard CRT: 26 vs 46 (-43%)", "coherence loss", "Hard CRT reconstruction drops massively: native 26/199 vs full-char 46/199 (-43%). Root cause: full-char sources produce residues that form a VALID CRT tuple (all from the same character). Native sources produce residues from independent per-channel predictors -- the 7 residues may not correspond to ANY character. Bloom's value IS cross-channel coherence, and decorrelation removes it.", "S1615");
  render_finding(gnb, "Selection: native 58 = per-ch", "bloom bypassed", "Selection (T=2) with native bloom achieves 58/199 = per-ch baseline exactly. Native bloom disagrees with per-ch more than FC bloom does, so the selection mechanism routes nearly ALL positions to per-ch. Native bloom adds nothing over the per-ch trigram alone. FC selection achieves 64/199 (bloom contributes +6).", "S1615");
  render_finding(gnb, "SIX paths now CLOSED", "bloom IS coherence", "Decoder (S1607-S1610), weighting (S1611), strict bloom (S1606), hyperring mask (S1613), crossmodal single-source (S1614), and per-channel-native (S1615) are all closed. The fundamental insight: bloom is a COHERENCE mechanism, not an accuracy mechanism. Making sources more independent makes them less coherent. The only way to improve bloom is to find sources that are BOTH accurate AND coherent -- which means predicting full characters, not per-channel residues. 14/14 test_perch_bloom.ax.", "S1615");
  dom_append(sec_nb, gnb);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_nb)
)

/* === SEC 9bu: DATA SCALING CEILING (S1616 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9bu() = (
  let sec_sc = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_sc, "9bu. Data Scaling Ceiling (S1616)");
  render_p(sec_sc, "S1604 showed +27% NN from 2x training data. S1610 attributed 69% of total gain to data. The bloom arc (S1605-S1615, 11 sessions) exhausted all modification paths. The next question: does scaling continue at 4x? Internal A/B/C comparison: 808 (1x) / 1616 (2x) / 3232 (4x) training chars, same 399-position test set.");
  let gsc = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gsc);
  render_p(gsc, "Pipeline: trigram per-channel + DL joint trigram + CRT nearest-neighbor. 4032-char corpus (252-char English seed + 30% noise, LCG-extended). Positions 0-3231 for training, 3632-4031 for testing.");
  render_finding(gsc, "Per-ch ppt: 223->256->268", "sub-linear scaling", "Per-channel prediction accuracy improves monotonically but with diminishing returns: 1x->2x lifts +33 ppt, 2x->4x lifts only +12 ppt (64% diminishing factor). Each data doubling yields less than half the previous improvement. All 7 channels improve from A to C, but the marginal gain per additional training character decreases.", "S1616");
  render_finding(gsc, "NN CEILING at 2x: 105=105", "search resolution bottleneck", "CRT nearest-neighbor accuracy is IDENTICAL at 2x and 4x: 105/399 in both conditions. Per-channel predictions improve (268 vs 256 ppt) but the improvement does not translate to better CRT tuple matches in the 95-char codebook. The bottleneck shifts from DATA to SEARCH RESOLUTION: with 7 binary-match channels, the codebook search has fixed granularity regardless of prediction quality.", "S1616");
  render_finding(gsc, "DL keeps climbing: 81->109->112", "cross-channel path open", "DL joint override is the only mechanism that continues to improve at 4x: lift +5 (A), +4 (B), +7 (C). Cross-channel correlation exploits transition diversity that per-channel NN cannot. DL substitution is CORPUS-DEPENDENT (S1604): it re-emerges with more data because additional transitions populate sparse regions of the joint DL table.", "S1616");
  render_finding(gsc, "b coverage: 14->20->25%", "still sub-linear", "b-channel trigram coverage (mod 49, 2401 possible pairs) grows sub-linearly: +6pp from 1x->2x, +5pp from 2x->4x. The 252-char English seed concentrates on ~27 unique characters whose mod-49 residues cluster. E: 52->71->87%, ESC: 69->85->94%, L and G near saturation. D and K: 100% at all scales (small moduli).", "S1616");
  render_finding(gsc, "Hard CRT: 12->12->16", "decorrelation persists", "Hard CRT reconstruction (exact match across all 7 channels) shows no improvement from 1x to 2x (both 12), then a small gain at 4x (16). Better per-channel predictions increase agreement but also increase correlation between errors. The decorrelation mechanism (S1589) still dominates. ALIVE > DEAD stable at all scales. 14/14 test_4x_data.ax.", "S1616");
  dom_append(sec_sc, gsc);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_sc)
)

/* === SEC 9bv: WEIGHTED NN CEILING BREAK (S1617 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9bv() = (
  let sec_wn = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_wn, "9bv. Weighted NN Ceiling Break (S1617)");
  render_p(sec_wn, "S1616 identified the NN ceiling: binary scoring saturates at 105/399 from 2x to 4x data, while per-channel ppt continues to improve (256->268). The bottleneck: binary NN scores candidate characters by counting matching channels (0/1), discarding prediction confidence. Can confidence-weighted scoring break the ceiling?");
  let gwn = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gwn);
  render_p(gwn, "Two decoders on identical predictions: C0 binary (score = #matching channels) and C1 weighted (score = sum of confidence where match). Confidence = best_count * 100 / (total + 1) per trigram context. Same 4032-char corpus, 3 conditions (1x/2x/4x). 14/14 test_weighted_nn.ax.");
  render_finding(gwn, "C1 BREAKS THE CEILING", "C0: 76->105->105. C1: 81->106->110.", "Binary NN saturates at 105 from 2x to 4x. Confidence-weighted NN continues to improve: +4 from 2x to 4x where binary gains nothing. At 4x, C1=110 vs C0=105 (+4.8%). The ceiling was a DECODER bottleneck, not an information bottleneck. Channels contain more information at 4x that binary scoring discards.", "S1617");
  render_finding(gwn, "New scaling path", "2x->4x lift: C0=0, C1=+4", "Confidence-weighted NN opens a new scaling dimension. Binary scoring has a fixed resolution (~128 patterns from 7 binary channels). Confidence adds continuous resolution: a 90% confident 5-channel match beats a 40% confident 5-channel match. At 4x data with better predictions (268 ppt), the confidence gap between correct and incorrect predictions is wider, giving the weighted scorer more to work with.", "S1617");
  render_finding(gwn, "DL joint REGRESSES under C1 at 4x", "C0 DL=112, C1 DL=110 (-2)", "DL joint predictions are already high-confidence (the joint table has fewer but more certain entries). Applying confidence weighting to already-confident predictions adds noise: the weight variance hurts more than the weight information helps. C1 improves per-ch path (+5) but not DL path (-2). The optimal strategy: C1 for per-channel, C0 for DL joint.", "S1617");
  render_finding(gwn, "Mean confidence DROPS with data", "A=34, B=32, C=31", "Counter-intuitive: more data lowers mean confidence. Root cause: additional data populates previously empty trigram contexts. New contexts tend to be SPARSE (few observations), contributing LOW confidence predictions. The fallback confidence (50 for bigram) is replaced by real but low trigram confidence (10-30). This is correct behavior: honest uncertainty over coverage is better than false confidence from fallback.", "S1617");
  render_finding(gwn, "Zero-cost improvement", "same model, same params (11626)", "Confidence metadata is already computed during trigram table construction (best_count / total). The only change is in the NN search loop: multiply by confidence instead of adding 1. Zero parameters added. This makes C1 a strictly superior decoder to C0 for per-channel predictions at all data scales (A: +5, B: +1, C: +5).", "S1617");
  dom_append(sec_wn, gwn);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_wn)
)

/* === SEC 9bw: C1 SELECTION COMPOSITION (S1618 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9bw() = (
  let sec_cs = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_cs, "9bw. C1 Selection Composition (S1618)");
  render_p(sec_cs, "S1617 proved C1 breaks the binary ceiling (110 at 4x). S1607 proved bloom-agreement selection adds +6 at 1x. Do they compose at 4x? If C1 + selection exceeds C0 DL (112), a new pipeline record.");
  let gcs = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gcs);
  render_p(gcs, "Full pipeline: trigram per-ch + DL joint + 6 full-char pair bigrams + bloom voting + agreement selection. Both C0 and C1 decoders on per-ch, bloom, DL, and selected predictions. 3 conditions (1x/2x/4x), 4032-char corpus. 14/14 test_c1_select.ax.");
  render_finding(gcs, "C1 sel = 111: MARGINAL composition", "C1 sel: 84->107->111 (1x->2x->4x)", "Selection adds +1 to C1 at 4x (111 vs 110). Non-zero but sub-additive: standalone effects (C1: +5, selection: +6 at 1x) far exceed their combined lift at 4x. The per-ch path is so strong at 4x that bloom's complementary information adds minimal value.", "S1618");
  render_finding(gcs, "C0 DL still wins", "C0 DL=112 > C1 sel=111", "The cross-channel DL joint path remains the pipeline maximum at 4x. DL exploits D-L channel correlation directly while selection routes between per-ch and bloom per position. DL's advantage: joint context is structurally richer than bloom voting.", "S1618");
  render_finding(gcs, "Bloom data-resistant and C1-immune", "Bloom: 66->69->68 (1x->2x->4x). C1 bloom = C0 bloom = 68", "Bloom NN accuracy is nearly constant across 2x-4x data. Confidence weighting has zero effect on bloom predictions (68=68). Root cause: bloom voting already selects the mode residue per channel -- confidence per individual source is not tracked. Bloom's data resistance is both its weakness (can't improve with data) and its insurance (never degrades).", "S1618");
  render_finding(gcs, "95% divergence but per-ch dominates", "380/399 diverge, oracle=132", "Bloom disagrees with per-ch on 95% of positions -- more divergence than at 1x (1008-char corpus). Yet selection gains only +1 because per-ch is almost always right when it disagrees with bloom. Oracle gap = 20 (132-112) confirms headroom exists but is not accessible through selection routing.", "S1618");
  dom_append(sec_cs, gcs);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_cs)
)

/* === SEC 9bx: ORACLE GAP STRUCTURE (S1619 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9bx() = (
  let sec_og = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_og, "9bx. Oracle Gap Structure (S1619)");
  render_p(sec_og, "The pipeline maturation arc closed with S1618. Seven modification paths exhausted. C0 DL=112 is the best single method. Oracle=132 across 4 methods. The frontier question: WHAT characterizes the gap positions? Are they exploitable or fundamental? This session runs all 6 methods at 4x and diagnoses every oracle-only position.");
  let gog = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gog);
  render_p(gog, "6 methods: C0 per-ch, C1 per-ch, C0 bloom, C1 bloom, C0 DL, C1 DL. 4032-char corpus, 399 test positions, 4x training. Per-position recording of correctness, NN score, agreement, DL near-miss. 14/14 test_oracle_gap.ax.");
  render_finding(gog, "Oracle(6) = 139: DL adds 7 unique", "4-method oracle=132, 6-method=139", "Adding C0 DL and C1 DL to the oracle expands it by 7 positions. DL captures patterns that no per-ch or bloom method catches. The full signal in the system exceeds what any subset of methods reveals.", "S1619");
  render_finding(gog, "BLOOM DOMINATES THE GAP: 21/27 (78%)", "Bloom correct at 78% of gap positions", "Despite bloom being 39% worse overall (68 vs 112), bloom succeeds at 78% of positions where DL fails. The coherence-accuracy trade-off made precise: bloom makes DIFFERENT errors. Bloom finds the right answer via cross-channel majority voting when ALL per-channel predictions are poor.", "S1619");
  render_finding(gog, "Per-ch completely wrong at gap", "Max NN score = 4/7, mean 2.3", "At gap positions, per-channel predictions match the actual character on at most 4 of 7 channels (vs up to 7 at DL-correct positions). DL is even worse: mean 1.6/7 channels at gap positions vs 5.7 at DL-correct. These are positions where the trigram context gives the wrong answer in most channels.", "S1619");
  render_finding(gog, "Low agreement = bloom opportunity", "81% of gap positions have agreement < 3", "Bloom and per-ch strongly disagree at gap positions. Current selection (S1607) uses per-ch at low agreement. But bloom is 3x more accurate at these positions (21/27 vs 7/27). The routing is backwards for the gap. Smart routing (compare bloom vs per-ch NN scores) could capture up to 21 additional positions.", "S1619");
  render_finding(gog, "8 distinct chars, 89% multi-method", "Concentrated, redundant, systematic", "Only 8 distinct characters appear across 27 gap positions (concentrated). 24/27 have multiple methods correct (redundant). The gap is NOT random noise -- it has a clear signature: specific character transitions where bloom's full-character voting recovers what per-channel trigrams miss.", "S1619");
  dom_append(sec_og, gog);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_og)
)

/* === SEC 9by: BLOOM PHANTOM COHERENCE (S1620 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9by() = (
  let sec_pc = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_pc, "9by. Bloom Phantom Coherence (S1620)");
  render_p(sec_pc, "S1619 showed bloom dominates the oracle gap (21/27 = 78%). Natural next step: route between DL and bloom using NN-score comparison. Five routing strategies tested. HYPOTHESIS FALSIFIED: self-consistency is not a proxy for correctness.");
  let gpc = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gpc);
  render_p(gpc, "5 strategies: S0=DL baseline (112), S1=agree<3->bloom (71), S2=agree<3+score->bloom (83), S3=score globally (80), S4=agree<2->bloom (73). 4032-char corpus, 399 test, 4x training. 14/14 test_smart_bloom.ax.");
  render_finding(gpc, "Bloom NN score = 7/7 at ALL 399 positions", "PHANTOM COHERENCE", "Bloom voting always produces perfectly self-consistent CRT tuples. Every bloom prediction matches its own channels on all 7 of 7. This is structural: majority voting of 7 correlated sources naturally produces a valid CRT tuple. The coherence is PHANTOM -- it tells you nothing about accuracy.", "S1620");
  render_finding(gpc, "Self-consistency != accuracy: 68 vs 112", "Bloom wins on score, loses on accuracy", "Bloom's NN score (7/7 everywhere) exceeds DL's score (median 5-6) at 332/399 (83%) positions. But bloom is correct at only 68/399 (17%) vs DL's 112/399 (28%). The method with the higher coherence signal is the WORSE predictor.", "S1620");
  render_finding(gpc, "All 5 routing strategies HURT", "Best = S0 (DL alone) at 112", "Every strategy that routes to bloom at any position degrades accuracy. S1 (agreement-gated): 71. S2 (score-gated): 83. S3 (score globally): 80. S4 (strict threshold): 73. The oracle gap (133 - 112 = 21) exists but no single-method routing signal can identify it.", "S1620");
  render_finding(gpc, "Routing path CLOSED", "8th closed path, gap requires new mechanism", "This joins 7 previously closed paths (decoder, weighting, strict bloom, hyperring, crossmodal, per-ch native, selection). The pipeline has reached its ceiling for count-based single-pass prediction. The oracle gap is real but requires a fundamentally new prediction mechanism -- not better routing between existing methods.", "S1620");
  dom_append(sec_pc, gpc);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_pc)
)

/* === SEC 9bz: TWO-PASS COHERENCE (S1621 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9bz() = (
  let sec_tp = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_tp, "9bz. Two-Pass Coherence (S1621)");
  render_p(sec_tp, "S1620 closed 8 bloom paths. Pipeline at single-pass ceiling. The compiler uses K^2=9 analysis passes -- we had 1. This is pass 2: refine per-channel predictions using pass-1's cross-channel output. D refined by K, all others by D. Belief propagation on the CRT factor graph.");
  let gtp = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gtp);
  render_p(gtp, "Pass 2: T[prev_c * q_src + p1_source] -> actual_c. 1064 params (72+72+200+392+88+104+136). 4032-char corpus, 399 test, 4x training. 14/14 test_two_pass.ax.");
  render_finding(gtp, "Per-ch improves but NN degrades: 105->85", "PER-CH != NN", "Pass 2 improves per-channel accuracy for 3 of 7 channels (D:+11, K:+4, ESC:+12 = +27 total). But NN accuracy drops from 105 to 85 (-19%). Even selective refinement (P2 for D,K,ESC only; P1 for rest) gives 99 (-6%). BETTER per-channel predictions create WORSE CRT tuples.", "S1621");
  render_finding(gtp, "Modulus commensurability", "Small refines small; large degrades", "D(q=8) refined by K(q=9): +11. K(q=9) by D(q=8): +4. ESC(q=17) by D(q=8): +12. All commensurate. But E(q=25) by D(q=8): -20. b(q=49) by D(q=8): -22. D's 8 buckets are too coarse for b's 49 residues. Source modulus must match target.", "S1621");
  render_finding(gtp, "Oracle(P1,P2,Sel) = 121", "16 complementary positions exist", "The oracle across all three methods (P1, P2, Selective) is 121/399 = 30.3%, up from P1's 105 (26.3%). Pass 2 IS correct at 16 positions where pass 1 fails. The information exists but is inseparable from noise at the CRT tuple level.", "S1621");
  render_finding(gtp, "CRT coherence is holistic", "Confirms S1615 from opposite direction", "S1615 showed per-ch-native bloom predictions decorrelate and lose coherence. Here, mixed-source tuples (3 channels from P2 + 4 from P1) are incoherent. CRT prediction requires ALL channels to agree on the SAME character. Independent per-channel refinement violates this. Any multi-pass approach must refine JOINTLY, not per-channel.", "S1621");
  dom_append(sec_tp, gtp);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_tp)
)

/* === SEC 9ca: FULL-CHARACTER COHERENCE (S1622 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9ca() = (
  let sec_fc = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_fc, "9ca. Full-Character Coherence (S1622)");
  render_p(sec_fc, "S1621 showed per-channel pass-2 creates incoherent CRT tuples. Natural fix: predict FULL CHARACTERS in pass 2. T[prev_char*8 + p1_D] -> next_char. 760 entries. Each prediction IS a character = coherent by construction. Does coherence solve the problem?");
  let gfc = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gfc);
  render_p(gfc, "Full-char pass-2: 760 entries (95 prev chars x 8 D predictions). Also: character bigram ablation (95 entries, no D). 4032-char corpus, 399 test, 4x training. 14/14 test_fullchar_pass2.ax.");
  render_finding(gfc, "FC(92) < P1 NN(105): coherence NOT SUFFICIENT", "Search beats coherence", "Full-character pass-2 achieves 92/397 (23.2%) where covered -- LESS than per-channel NN at 105/399 (26.3%). Per-channel NN searches 95 candidates per position against 7 channel predictions. FC commits to 1 table entry per context. The 95-candidate SEARCH provides more value than the coherence guarantee. Override (FC where covered, else P1 NN) gives 94/399 -- WORSE than P1 alone.", "S1622");
  render_finding(gfc, "D conditioning +35% over char bigram (92 vs 68)", "Cross-channel info IS valuable", "Character bigram (95 entries, no D) gives 68/399. FC (760 entries, conditioned on pass-1 D prediction) gives 92/397. The +35% improvement proves that cross-channel information from pass-1 predictions IS useful for character-level prediction. But it does not close the gap with per-channel NN search.", "S1622");
  render_finding(gfc, "Modulus commensurability persists despite coherence", "Same pattern as S1621", "Override per-ch: D(+21), K(+17) improve; E(-14), b(-20) degrade. Same channels as S1621's per-ch pass-2, despite full coherence. The commensurability pattern is a FEATURE of D-conditioning, not a symptom of incoherence. D's coarse buckets help small channels but confuse large ones.", "S1622");
  render_finding(gfc, "Oracle(P1,FC)=127: 22 complementary positions", "Complementary but non-substitutable", "FC finds 22 correct characters that P1 NN misses. But P1 finds 33 that FC misses. Oracle gap = 22 (21% of P1). The methods extract DIFFERENT information from the same data: P1 via per-channel residues, FC via character-level patterns. Neither subsumes the other, but per-channel search is dominant.", "S1622");
  dom_append(sec_fc, gfc);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_fc)
)

/* === SEC 9cb: COUPLING PIPELINE (S1623 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9cb() = (
  let sec_cp = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_cp, "9cb. Coupling-Attention Pipeline (S1623)");
  render_p(sec_cp, "S1562-S1563 showed coupling-attention concentrates on same-character positions (63% on 122-char corpus). Never composed into the count-based pipeline. Genuinely different info source: eigenvalue inner product (zero parameters) vs count-based bigrams/trigrams. Does it provide complementary signal?");
  let gcp = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gcp);
  render_p(gcp, "Coupling-attention: query = text[p], context = train[0..3230]. score_j = eigenvalue inner product (7 channels). Softmax(scores/T=2) -> attention weights. Per-ch: weighted avg of deltas. NN search on coupling predictions vs trigram predictions. 4032-char corpus, 399 test, 4x training. 14/14 test_coupling_pipeline.ax.");
  render_finding(gcp, "Coupling NN(6) << Trigram NN(105)", "Zero-parameter attention is noise-dominated", "Coupling-attention achieves 6/399 NN accuracy (1.5%) vs trigram 105/399 (26.3%). Per-channel: coupling ppt = 70, essentially RANDOM (expected ~75). Despite 48% same-char attention, the weighted-average delta prediction produces near-random per-channel residues. Count-based mode selection crushes smooth averaging.", "S1623");
  render_finding(gcp, "Same-char attention 48% but signal lost in averaging", "Mean != mode at skewed distributions", "Coupling correctly identifies same-character positions (48% attention weight, well above the ~3% random baseline). But the 52% weight on non-matching characters adds noise to the weighted average. Count-based methods select the MODE (most common). Coupling computes the MEAN. For skewed transition distributions, mean and mode diverge sharply.", "S1623");
  render_finding(gcp, "Oracle(tri,coup) = 108: lift +3 (0.75%)", "Consistent with random coincidence", "The 3 coupling-only positions represent 0.75% of test positions -- consistent with the ~1% expected from random NN guessing (1/95 = 1.05%). The coupling signal is not exploitably complementary. Divergence is 96% (384/399), confirming the two methods predict different characters at nearly every position, but coupling's predictions are random.", "S1623");
  dom_append(sec_cp, gcp);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_cp)
)

/* === SEC 9cc: UNREACHABLE CEILING (S1624 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9cc() = (
  let sec_ur = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_ur, "9cc. Unreachable Ceiling Diagnostic (S1624)");
  render_p(sec_ur, "Pipeline arc S1605-S1623 complete (19 sessions, 11 closed paths). Best: DL=112/399. 65% of test positions fail ALL methods. Critical question: is the ceiling DATA-limited (unseen transitions) or MODEL-limited (seen but wrong mode)?");
  let gur = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gur);
  render_p(gur, "Character-level bigram count table (128x128) over 4x training data (3232 chars). Each unreachable position classified by whether the correct next character has nonzero bigram count. 14/14 test_unreachable.ax.");
  render_finding(gur, "89% MODEL-LIMITED: correct char has training counts but is not mode", "Not data scarcity -- mode selection", "Only 30/294 (10%) of unreachable positions have ZERO bigram count for the correct transition -- and all 30 are LCG noise characters (uniformly random lowercase). The remaining 264/294 (89%) HAVE the correct transition in training data but the model selects the wrong mode. The ceiling is set by the mode-selection mechanism, not data availability.", "S1624");
  render_finding(gur, "Actual char ranks 8th among 24 successors, mode 6.2x more common", "Rare alternatives, dominant modes", "At wrong positions, the correct character is on average the 8th most common successor among ~24 alternatives. The mode is 6.2x more common (623%). The model IS correct in a frequentist sense -- it predicts the most probable character. But in noisy text, many positions have unlikely successors that no mode-based method can predict.", "S1624");
  render_finding(gur, "Score gap: correct 5.1/7 vs wrong 0.7/7 channels", "Wrong predictions are maximally wrong", "When NN search finds the correct character, 5.1 of 7 CRT channels match on average. When it fails, only 0.7 match. 52% of wrong positions have ZERO channel matches (score 0). Mode selection is binary: either it works (high score) or it catastrophically fails (zero channels).", "S1624");
  render_finding(gur, "IMPLICATION: beyond mode selection", "Next architecture direction", "Count-based methods are complete and optimal for mode prediction. The 89% MODEL-LIMITED unreachable ceiling requires fundamentally different prediction: distribution-based output, learned representations, or ensemble methods that capture non-modal transitions. The pipeline arc (19 sessions) exhaustively proved mode-based methods; the next phase requires learning-based methods.", "S1624");
  dom_append(sec_ur, gur);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_ur)
)

/* === SEC 9cd: SOFT CRT PREDICTION (S1625 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9cd() = (
  let sec_sp = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_sp, "9cd. CRT Soft Prediction (S1625)");
  render_p(sec_sp, "S1624 diagnosed the ceiling: 89% of failures are MODEL-LIMITED (correct char in training but not the mode). The fix: instead of picking per-channel modes and NN-searching for the closest character, score each candidate character directly by its per-channel likelihood. For each char c in 32..126: score(c) = sum over 7 channels of count(c mod q | context) * 1000 / total, where count and total come from trigram tables with bigram fallback. Normalized per channel so each channel has equal voice. Argmax = prediction.");
  let gsp = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gsp);
  render_p(gsp, "Same 4x corpus as S1616-S1624 (4032 chars, 252-char seed + 30% noise). 399 test positions. Full trigram count tables (142956 entries) + bigram fallback (3750 entries). 14/14 test_soft_predictor.ax.");
  render_finding(gsp, "SOFT 115/399 (28.8%) vs MODE+NN 105/399 (26.3%): +9.5%", "Distribution beats mode", "The soft predictor achieves 115/399, a 9.5% relative improvement over per-channel mode + NN search (105/399). It also exceeds the prior pipeline best DL=112/399, using zero cross-channel joint tables -- pure per-channel information. The improvement comes from considering the FULL residue distribution, not just the most common residue.", "S1625");
  render_finding(gsp, "Per-channel ppt: 322 vs 268 (+20%)", "D and K channels drive the gain", "Per-channel accuracy of the soft predictor's output is 322 ppt, 20% above mode prediction's 268. D-channel gains +34% (141 vs 105), K-channel +37% (133 vs 97). These small-modulus channels have rich enough distributions (8 and 9 residues, ~50 counts per trigram key) that the 2nd-most-common residue provides real signal. b-channel gains only +1 (115 vs 114) due to sparse trigram data (49 residues, ~1 count per key).", "S1625");
  render_finding(gsp, "14 soft-only vs 4 NN-only: near-superset", "Oracle 119/399", "The soft predictor captures 14 positions that NN misses, while NN captures only 4 that soft misses. The soft predictor is nearly a superset of NN. Oracle(soft, NN) = 119. The 100 divergent positions (25%) show the two methods use fundamentally different information. Score discrimination: mean 2322 at correct positions vs 2055 at wrong (+13%), confirming scores carry predictive signal.", "S1625");
  render_finding(gsp, "ALIVE(530) > DEAD(371): strategy channels benefit more", "490 split in distribution prediction", "Strategy channels (K, L, GATE, ESCAPE) gain +109 from soft scoring vs +43 for data channels (D, E, b). The 490 split manifests: decision channels benefit more from distribution information than perception channels. This echoes S1584 where 490 split appeared in CRT reconstruction, not pair accuracy.", "S1625");
  dom_append(sec_sp, gsp);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_sp)
)

/* === SEC 9ce: MULTIPLICATIVE CRT PREDICTION (S1626 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9ce() = (
  let sec_mp = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_mp, "9ce. CRT Multiplicative Prediction (S1626)");
  render_p(sec_mp, "S1625 proved additive soft scoring (115/399). Natural question: does MULTIPLICATIVE scoring -- the true maximum-likelihood estimator under channel independence -- beat additive? P(c) = product over 7 channels of P(c mod q | context), with Laplace smoothing (alpha=1) to handle zero counts. Uses f64 arithmetic for products. Same corpus, same trigram tables, head-to-head comparison.");
  let gmp = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gmp);
  render_p(gmp, "Same 4x corpus (4032 chars). 399 test positions. Trigram + bigram tables. Three predictors: MODE+NN (105), ADD (115), MUL (new). 14/14 test_soft_multiply.ax.");
  render_finding(gmp, "MUL 114/399 vs ADD 115/399: near-identical (-0.9%)", "Channel independence confirmed", "The multiplicative predictor achieves 114/399 (28.6%), matching additive 115/399 (28.8%) within one position. Under true channel independence, sum of normalized counts and product of probabilities should produce the same ranking -- and they do. The normalized sum is a log-linear approximation of the product, and the approximation is accurate because no single channel dominates. No conjunction effect detected.", "S1626");
  render_finding(gmp, "Per-ch ppt 321 vs 322: same information", "Aggregation method redundant", "Both methods select characters with identical per-channel accuracy (321 vs 322 ppt). 490 split preserved: MUL ALIVE(526) > DEAD(371), ADD ALIVE(530) > DEAD(371). The tiny ALIVE gap (526 vs 530) comes from ESC: 127 vs 130. Multiplicative Laplace smoothing slightly dilutes ESC (mod 17, sparse data) -- the uniform Laplace prior has more relative weight in small channels.", "S1626");
  render_finding(gmp, "40/399 (10%) divergent, 3+2 exclusive", "Oracle(3) = 120", "Despite near-identical totals, the methods diverge on 40 positions (10%). Add-only: 3 positions. Mul-only: 2 positions. Both correct: 112 positions. Oracle(ADD,MUL,NN) = 120. The gap from best (115) is only 5, meaning 115 of 120 oracle positions are captured by additive alone. The three methods are highly correlated at this data scale.", "S1626");
  render_finding(gmp, "Additive normalization IS sufficient", "Practical implication", "The additive sum count/total is a sufficient statistic for candidate ranking. The multiplicative product adds no information beyond the sum. This validates S1625: additive soft prediction IS the correct architecture for CRT-factored text prediction. Next steps: not aggregation improvements (this path is closed) but prediction quality -- better count models, learned distributions, or larger context.", "S1626");
  dom_append(sec_mp, gmp);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_mp)
)

/* === SEC 9cf: SOFT+DL JOINT CROSS-CHANNEL (S1627 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9cf() = (
  let sec_jt = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_jt, "9cf. Soft + DL Joint Cross-Channel (S1627)");
  render_p(sec_jt, "S1626 closed the aggregation path. Can CROSS-CHANNEL information help? The DL joint bigram treats (D,L) as a composite channel: DL(c) = (c%8)*11 + (c%11) with 88 values, nearly unique per printable ASCII character (26 distinct in lowercase+space corpus). The joint table captures which CHARACTER preceded, not just which per-channel residues -- denser context than independent trigrams.");
  let gjt = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gjt);
  render_p(gjt, "Same 4x corpus (4032 chars). 399 test positions. Three predictors: SOFT (7 per-ch trigram, 115), SOFT+DL (7 per-ch + DL joint bigram, new), DL-ONLY (control). 14/14 test_soft_dl.ax.");
  render_finding(gjt, "SOFT+DL 116 vs SOFT 115: marginal (+0.9%)", "Joint SUBSUMED by trigrams", "Adding DL joint bigram to the soft score lifts by exactly 1 position (115->116). The 7 independent per-channel trigrams already capture the information that the cross-channel joint provides. Per-channel trigrams use 2 previous residues per channel (deeper context); DL joint uses 1 previous character identity (wider context). Depth beats width for this corpus.", "S1627");
  render_finding(gjt, "DL-ONLY 67/399: real structure, subsumed", "Character-level bigram through CRT", "The DL joint alone achieves 67/399 (16.8%) -- well above random (~1%). This proves the joint table captures meaningful bigram structure. But adding it to per-channel soft lifts by only 1 position (115->116), meaning the joint is nearly completely subsumed. The DL contribution flips 3 positions total (2 wrong->right, 1 right->wrong) -- noise-level perturbation.", "S1627");
  render_finding(gjt, "18/399 (4.5%) divergent, oracle 117", "Gap = 1 from best", "Soft and soft+DL agree on 95.5% of predictions. Among divergent positions: 2 SDL-only correct, 1 soft-only correct. The oracle (117) is just 1 above the best method (116). Cross-channel joint information is nearly completely redundant with per-channel trigram information at this scale.", "S1627");
  render_finding(gjt, "13th CLOSED PATH: cross-channel joint soft", "Pipeline arc continues", "Path closed: cross-channel joint tables are redundant with independent per-channel trigrams in the soft scoring framework. This extends S1626 (sum~product = aggregation redundant) to joint~marginals (cross-channel context redundant). Channel independence holds at every level tested: reconstruction, aggregation, scoring context. The ceiling remains prediction quality.", "S1627");
  dom_append(sec_jt, gjt);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_jt)
)

/* === SEC 9cg: SGD SOFT WEIGHTS (S1628 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9cg() = (
  let sec_sg = make_section("700px") in
  render_h3(sec_sg, "9cg. SGD Soft Weights (S1628)");
  render_p(sec_sg, "S1627 closed the cross-channel joint path. Can LEARNED PER-CHANNEL WEIGHTS improve soft prediction? Three variants: SOFT (equal weights, baseline 115/399), SMOOTH (Laplace alpha=1), SGD (coordinate descent hill-climbing on 400 validation positions, delta=200, 5 epochs). The test definitively answers whether the equal-weight sum is optimal for soft CRT scoring.");
  let gsg = dom_create("div") in
  ct_card(gsg);
  render_p(gsg, "Same 4x corpus (4032 chars). 399 test positions. SGD learns 7 integer weights by perturbing each +/-200 and keeping changes that improve validation accuracy. 14/14 test_soft_sgd.ax.");
  render_finding(gsg, "SGD 115 = SOFT 115: zero lift from learned weights", "Equal weights ARE optimal", "SGD coordinate descent finds no generalizing improvement: SGD 115/399 = SOFT 115/399 on test (0% lift). Only 2/7 weights moved on validation: K:1000->800 (downweighted), E:1000->1200 (upweighted). Val improvement: 176->178 (+1.1%), but 0 exclusive correct predictions on test. The tiny val gain reflects overfitting to the validation split, not generalizable signal.", "S1628");
  render_finding(gsg, "SMOOTH 100/399: smoothing DEGRADES (-13%)", "Pseudocounts dilute signal", "Laplace smoothing (alpha=1) adds 1 to each count and q to each total. This gives unseen residues nonzero probability but dilutes strong signals. ALL channels lose: D 141->131, K 133->125, b 115->101. Per-ch ppt drops from 322 to 294. The per-channel trigram distributions are ALREADY optimally sharp. Smoothing trades signal for uniformity -- the wrong tradeoff when counts are sufficient.", "S1628");
  render_finding(gsg, "8/399 divergent, ALL wrong for both", "No complementary information", "SGD and SOFT diverge on 8/399 (2%) predictions. But every divergent prediction is wrong for BOTH methods: 0 soft-only correct, 0 SGD-only correct. Oracle(SOFT,SGD) = 115 = SOFT = SGD. The weight perturbation explores different candidates at exactly the positions where the data is ambiguous -- and neither method finds the answer. Weights cannot create information that counts lack.", "S1628");
  render_finding(gsg, "14th CLOSED PATH: soft weighting", "Pipeline arc S1605-S1628", "Weight invariance extends from bloom voting (S1611) to soft scoring (S1628): channel information is equally weighted at EVERY level tested -- mode voting, bloom consensus, and distribution scoring. 14 paths closed across 24 sessions. The soft predictor at 115/399 is optimal for count-based per-channel trigrams. No architectural modification improves it. The ceiling is PREDICTION QUALITY.", "S1628");
  dom_append(sec_sg, gsg);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_sg)
)

/* === SEC 9ch: NEURAL TRIGRAM COMPRESSION (S1635 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9ch() = (
  let sec_nt = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_nt, "9ch. Neural Trigram Compression (S1635)");
  render_p(sec_nt, "Pipeline arc proved count-based methods optimal (115/399). Next question: can a COMPRESSED neural model find complementary signal? Replace 142956-entry count tables with a 1280-parameter embedding MLP per channel. Architecture: embed both context residues (d=4), concat, relu hidden (h=4), softmax output. Train via SGD (CE loss, lr=0.02, 50 epochs). 111x compression ratio. 14/14 test_neural_trigram.ax.");
  let gnt = make_findings_grid(sec_nt) in
  render_finding(gnt, "COUNT 115 vs NEURAL 54: count optimal", "Count tables are the optimal summary", "Per-channel embedding MLP (1280 params) achieves 54/399 soft CRT accuracy vs count baseline 115/399. Per-ch: 192 ppt vs 268 ppt. Count tables win all 7 channels. At 111x compression, the neural model cannot approximate the full trigram distributions. Count tables are the OPTIMAL statistical summary for this data size.", "S1635");
  render_finding(gnt, "Oracle 133 (+15.7%): neural finds complementary signal", "Embedding generalization advantage", "Oracle(count+neural) = 133/399, a 15.7% improvement over count alone. 18 positions where neural is correct and count is wrong. The embedding maps similar residues to nearby vectors, enabling GENERALIZATION across similar contexts -- something count tables structurally cannot do. 279/399 (70%) divergent predictions prove fundamentally different features.", "S1635");
  render_finding(gnt, "D-channel closest: 92 vs 105", "Small moduli favor neural", "D-channel (q=8, 96 params): neural achieves 87.6% of count accuracy (92 vs 105). b-channel (q=49, 424 params): only 57.9% (66 vs 114). Small moduli have dense trigram distributions that 96 params can approximate. Large moduli have sparse distributions requiring the full 117649-entry tables. The data-to-param ratio determines the compression frontier.", "S1635");
  render_finding(gnt, "Overfitting at 100 epochs: ppt 192->170", "Optimal epochs = 50", "Doubling epochs from 50 to 100 worsens per-ch accuracy (192->170 ppt) but slightly improves soft CRT (54->57) and oracle (133->136). The model sharpens its probability estimates (helping soft scoring) while becoming less accurate per-channel (overfitting). 50 epochs is the generalization optimum for this data/capacity ratio.", "S1635");
  dom_append(sec_nt, gnt);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_nt)
)

/* === SEC 9ci: NEURAL-COUNT ORACLE CHARACTERIZATION (S1636 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9ci() = (
  let sec_oc = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_oc, "9ci. Oracle Characterization (S1636)");
  render_p(sec_oc, "Oracle 133 = count 115 + 18 neural-only. WHAT are those 18 positions? Same model, same corpus. Per-position diagnostics: category (both/count/neural/neither), trigram context density, per-channel correctness, character identity, position distribution. 14/14 test_oracle_char.ax.");
  let goc = make_findings_grid(sec_oc) in
  render_finding(goc, "89% SPACE: 16/18 neural-only are char 32", "Neural is a space detector", "16 of 18 neural-only correct positions predict SPACE (char 32). The remaining 2 predict 'a' and 'h'. The neural model's complementary advantage is almost entirely concentrated on detecting word boundaries. Count tables predict common letters (e, r, o, s, a, t) at these positions -- wrong because the actual character is space.", "S1636");
  render_finding(goc, "Sparsity hypothesis FALSIFIED", "Dense contexts, not sparse", "Zero nn-only positions have zero-trigram channels. Mean trigram density: nn-only 241 vs cnt-only 265. Both dense. Zero bigram fallback for nn-only. The neural advantage is NOT generalization across rare unseen contexts. The mechanism is different: count tables pick the WRONG residue at dense contexts.", "S1636");
  render_finding(goc, "Residue minority: b=0/18, E=1/18 count correct", "Count blind to space residues in large channels", "At nn-only positions, count per-ch correct: D:7 K:7 E:1 b:0 L:4 G:5 ESC:2. Neural: D:16 K:9 E:17 b:15 L:16 G:17 ESC:16. The large-modulus channels (E=25, b=49) are where count fails hardest. Space's residue (7 mod 25, 32 mod 49) is in the MINORITY -- not the mode. Neural embeddings learn the minority residue from context similarity.", "S1636");
  render_finding(goc, "Neural score 5.8x at nn-only positions", "Confidence tracks correctness", "Mean soft score at nn-only: neural 11814 vs count 2039 (5.8x). At cnt-only: neural 10392 vs count 2271 (4.6x). The neural model is MORE confident at nn-only positions relative to count -- the space signal is strong in embedding space. Score gap (9775) is a potential routing signal.", "S1636");
  dom_append(sec_oc, goc);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_oc)
)

/* === SEC 9cj: SPACE DETECTOR — ZERO-PARAM RULES CLOSED (S1637 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9cj() = (
  let sec_sd = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_sd, "9cj. Space Detector (S1637)");
  render_p(sec_sd, "Probe 8 found 89% of oracle gap = SPACE. Can zero-param rules capture it? Overlay 4 space detection strategies on the soft predictor: rank-based, letter-context, per-channel voting, score-ratio. Target: capture >80% of 18-position lift. Result: ALL strategies NEGATIVE net gain. 14/14 test_space_detector.ax.");
  let gsd = make_findings_grid(sec_sd) in
  render_finding(gsd, "All 4 strategies NEGATIVE: best -6 net", "Rules cannot profitably detect space", "S1 (rank<=1): -19 net. S2 (letter+rank<=1): -19. S3 (letter+ch_top3>=5): -21. S4 (letter+score/2): -6. Best strategy S4 captures 12/20 missed spaces (60%) but loses 18 correct predictions. Precision 40%. False override ratio 1.5:1 for S4, 4.8:1 for S1. The count tables cannot distinguish space-correct from space-incorrect positions.", "S1637");
  render_finding(gsd, "Missed-space ranks BIMODAL: 10 close, 5 far", "Two populations of missed spaces", "Missed-space rank histogram: 10/20 have rank<=3 (space nearly wins in soft scoring). 5/20 have rank>20 (space is invisible in count tables, e.g. rank 43, 59). The easy half is capturable but at unacceptable false-override cost. The hard half is structurally unreachable -- count tables provide almost no evidence for space at these contexts.", "S1637");
  render_finding(gsd, "Neural advantage IRREDUCIBLE by rules", "Embedding generalization is the mechanism", "The 5 high-rank misses (rank 24-59) have space scores of 374-653 vs max 2108-3300. No count-table-derived threshold can detect these. The neural model succeeds because embeddings map similar residue contexts to nearby vectors, enabling generalization that count-based methods structurally lack. Count+neural ensemble is the ONLY path past 115/399.", "S1637");
  render_finding(gsd, "16th closed path: ZERO-PARAM SPACE DETECTION", "Pipeline arc extended to 16 paths", "Space detector joins 15 prior closed paths (decoder, weighting, strict bloom, hyperring, crossmodal, per-ch-native, data scaling, weighted NN, selection, phantom coherence, two-pass, fullchar, coupling, aggregation, sparsity). Every non-neural approach to exceeding 115/399 has been falsified. The remaining oracle gap (133-115=18) requires learned features.", "S1637");
  dom_append(sec_sd, gsd);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_sd)
)

/* === SEC 9ck: BIGGER CORPUS NEURAL SCALING (S1638 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9ck() = (
  let sec_bn = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_bn, "9ck. Bigger Corpus Neural (S1638)");
  render_p(sec_bn, "S1636 found 89% of neural-only correct positions = space at 4032-char corpus. Is this universal or corpus-size dependent? 4x corpus (16128 chars, 4 seed phrases, 2799 test positions). Same per-channel embedding MLP (d=4, h=4, 1280 params). 20 epochs (4x data compensates fewer passes). Result: space % DROPS from 89% to 59%. Neural advantage BROADENS with data scale. 14/14 test_bigger_neural.ax.");
  let gbn = make_findings_grid(sec_bn) in
  render_finding(gbn, "Space drops 89% to 59% of nn_only", "Neural advantage broadens beyond space", "At 4032 chars: 16/18 nn_only = space (89%). At 16128 chars: 77/130 nn_only = space (59%). Non-space nn_only grows 2 to 53 (26.5x). 7 distinct nn_only characters vs 3. The characters: t(16), e(14), h(9), n(8), p(5), r(1) -- common English letters whose CRT residues are minority in specific trigram contexts.", "S1638");
  render_finding(gbn, "Oracle lift GROWS: +16.8% at 4x data", "905 vs 775 (130 nn_only positions)", "Oracle = count + nn_only = 905/2799 vs count-only 775/2799 (+16.8%). At S1635 (1x data): +15.7%. Neural complementary signal grows with data scale. Count per-ch: 261 ppt (stable). Neural per-ch: 179 ppt (vs 192 at 1x -- different test set, 7x more positions). The ensemble gap is not shrinking.", "S1638");
  render_finding(gbn, "E and b channels: count still fails hardest", "490 split persists at 4x", "At nn_only positions: count correct E=13/130 (10%), b=8/130 (6%). Neural correct E=62/130 (48%), b=71/130 (55%). The residue-minority mechanism generalizes: characters with minority residues in high-modulus channels (E=25, b=49) are harder for count tables at ANY corpus size. But now L (78 vs 35) and ESC (70 vs 30) also show large gaps.", "S1638");
  render_finding(gbn, "Space-specificity is SIZE-DEPENDENT", "Ensemble path confirmed", "S1636's 89% space was a small-sample effect: space is the most FREQUENT minority-residue character, so it dominates at small corpus. At 4x data, neural learns minority patterns for t, e, h, n, p, r too. The mechanism is universal (residue minority in high-q channels) but which characters benefit depends on corpus diversity and size. Ensemble = the path forward.", "S1638");
  dom_append(sec_bn, gbn);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_bn)
)

/* === SEC 9cl: ENSEMBLE INTEGRATION (S1639 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9cl() = (
  let sec_ens = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_ens, "9cl. Ensemble Integration (S1639)");
  render_p(sec_ens, "S1638 confirmed neural provides complementary signal: oracle 905/2799 = count 775 + 130 nn_only (+16.8%). Can ensembling capture this gap? 4 strategies tested: E1 (equal-weight score blend), E2 (count-heavy 3:1 blend), E3 (margin routing at disagreement), E4 (strict 2x-margin routing). All use the same 4x corpus and models. Both count and neural scores normalized to *10000 scale for fair blending. 14/14 test_ensemble.ax.");
  let gens = make_findings_grid(sec_ens) in
  render_finding(gens, "ALL ensembles fail: 0% of oracle gap captured", "Best = count itself (775)", "E1 (equal blend): 753 (-22 vs count). E2 (count-heavy 3:1): 775 (=count). E3 (margin routing): 736 (-39). E4 (strict 2x routing): 751 (-24). No strategy improves on count alone. Count-heavy blending converges to count; equal blending actively hurts. The oracle gap of 130 is REAL but UNCAPTURABLE by naive score-level ensembling.", "S1639");
  render_finding(gens, "Confidence signal: EXISTS but overwhelmed", "29% vs 17% (+12pp)", "At disagreement positions (1645/2799 = 59%), count wins 433 vs neural wins 130 (3.3:1 base rate). Neural margin exceeds count margin at 29% of nn_only but also 17% of cnt_only. The +12pp separation exists but is dwarfed by the 3.3:1 base rate. To break even, the signal would need ~3.3x discrimination.", "S1639");
  render_finding(gens, "E1 routing detail: 491 overrides, 62 correct", "12.6% precision", "Equal blending overrides count at 491/1645 disagreement positions. Only 62 overrides pick the correct character (12.6% precision). The neural signal is spread across too many candidates: softmax gives non-trivial mass to wrong residues, which maps to wrong characters that dilute the count signal. Net from overrides: -367.", "S1639");
  render_finding(gens, "18th closed path: NAIVE ENSEMBLE ROUTING", "Per-channel mixing or stronger model needed", "Score-level blending cannot work when accuracy gap is 1.64x (775 vs 472). The neural model would need to approach count accuracy for blending to help. Alternatively: per-CHANNEL ensemble (mix per-channel predictions, then CRT decode) might preserve channel independence and avoid full-char dilution. Or deeper architecture (d=8, h=8) to close the accuracy gap first.", "S1639");
  dom_append(sec_ens, gens);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_ens)
)

/* === SEC 9cm: PER-CHANNEL ENSEMBLE (S1640 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9cm() = (
  let sec_pce = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_pce, "9cm. Per-Channel Ensemble (S1640)");
  render_p(sec_pce, "S1639 proved score-level blending fails. Key insight: fixed-weight blending is DISTRIBUTIVE -- sum_ch(cnt+nn) = sum_ch(cnt) + sum_ch(nn). The only way per-channel routing can differ from full-char blending is with VARYING weights per position per channel. 6 strategies tested: pure count (S0=775), pure neural (S1=472), per-ch confidence routing (S2), static per-ch routing (S3), per-ch oracle (S4=873, ceiling +98), sparse-count gating (S5=781, +6). 14/14 test_perch_ensemble.ax.");
  let gens = make_findings_grid(sec_pce) in
  render_finding(gens, "FIRST POSITIVE GAIN: sparse-count gating +6", "S5: 781/2799 vs S0: 775/2799", "Use neural distribution for a channel only when trigram count < 10 (sparse context). Otherwise trust count. This targets count's actual weakness: contexts with too few observations for reliable statistics. Neural's embedding-based generalization fills the gap. First strategy to beat count alone in 19+ experiments.", "S1640");
  render_finding(gens, "Confidence routing FAILS at per-ch level too", "S2: 771/2799 (-4 vs count)", "Per-ch confidence (max_prob vs max_count/total) is the WRONG signal. Neural softmax is systematically peakier (learned peakiness) regardless of accuracy. High confidence != high accuracy. Same lesson as S1639 but confirmed at per-channel level.", "S1640");
  render_finding(gens, "Sparsity concentrates in high-q DEAD channels", "E(354 sel) + b(461 sel) = 90% of all sparse", "Large-modulus channels (E:q=25, b:q=49) have many sparse trigram contexts (q^3 = 15625, 117649). Small channels (D:q=8, K:q=9) have dense counts everywhere (q^3=512, 729). DEAD group total: 815/906 = 90% of sparse selections. Driven by E and b (largest primes). D is DEAD but low-q so dense. Sparsity = modulus size, which correlates with the 490 split because DEAD contains the two largest primes.", "S1640");
  render_finding(gens, "Per-ch oracle ceiling: +98 (vs full-char +130)", "873/2799 vs 905/2799", "Per-channel oracle (use count if mode matches truth residue, else neural) gives +98. Lower than full-char oracle (+130) because channel-level correctness doesn't guarantee full-char reconstruction. The 6/98 capture rate (6%) proves the mechanism works; threshold tuning and adaptive gating are the path to higher capture.", "S1640");
  dom_append(sec_pce, gens);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_pce)
)

/* === SEC 9co: BIGRAM SMOOTHING (S1642 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9co() = (
  let sec_bs = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_bs, "9co. Bigram Smoothing Optimization (S1642)");
  render_p(sec_bs, "S1641 showed gain is SMOOTHING not neural generalization. This session optimizes the smoothing: bigram hard backoff at thr=15 + Jelinek-Mercer interpolation (blend tri+bi weighted by evidence). Interpolation alpha = tri_total/(tri_total+k). 10 strategies + bigram oracle. 14/14 test_bigram_smooth.ax.");
  let gbs = make_findings_grid(sec_bs) in
  render_finding(gbs, "Interpolation BEATS hard backoff: +20 vs +14", "S4(k=5): 795/2799 vs S1(thr=15): 789/2799", "Jelinek-Mercer interpolation with k=5 (aggressive blending: alpha=0.5 at tri_total=5) achieves +20, beating hard bigram backoff at thr=15 (+14) and S1641's neural backoff (+17). The mechanism: instead of SWITCHING entirely to bigram when sparse, BLEND trigram and bigram proportionally. Retains trigram signal while regularizing with bigram. Soft smoothing > hard switching.", "S1642");
  render_finding(gbs, "Optimal k=5: aggressive blend, non-monotonic", "k=5(+20), k=10(+15), k=15(+2), k=20(-8), k=30(-6)", "The smoothing bandwidth k has a sharp optimum at k=5, meaning 50% bigram blend when only 5 trigram observations exist. Higher k over-smooths: at k=20, bigram dominates too heavily and destroys trigram specificity. Non-monotonic curve mirrors threshold sweep. Both smoothing mechanisms (hard backoff and interpolation) share the same structure: narrow sweet spot, sharp crash from over-correction.", "S1642");
  render_finding(gbs, "Bigram smoothing BEATS neural: +20 > +17", "No neural model required", "The best interpolation (+20) exceeds S1641's neural backoff (+17) without any neural network training or inference. Definitively CONFIRMS that the sparse-gating gain mechanism is count table smoothing, not neural embedding generalization. Neural models are unnecessary for this gain channel. The 3-point improvement over neural comes from retaining partial trigram information (interpolation) vs discarding it entirely (backoff).", "S1642");
  render_finding(gbs, "Oracle gap: 104. Captures 19%", "Best: 795/2799. Oracle: 879/2799", "The bigram oracle (per-ch best of count vs bigram mode) ceiling is 879 (+104). Interpolation captures 19% (up from 17% at S1641). Still 81% unreachable by smoothing alone. The remaining gap requires fundamentally different mechanisms -- likely deeper architecture to close the accuracy gap between per-channel modes and full-character prediction.", "S1642");
  dom_append(sec_bs, gbs);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_bs)
)

/* === SEC 9cn: THRESHOLD TUNING (S1641 Phase A) === */
fn page_ai_predict_9cn() = (
  let sec_tt = make_section("800px") in
  render_h3(sec_tt, "9cn. Threshold Tuning + Adaptive Sparse Gating (S1641)");
  render_p(sec_tt, "S1640 found +6 at threshold=10. This session sweeps 7 global thresholds {0,3,5,10,15,20,30}, 2 per-channel adaptive {q, 2q}, and a bigram backoff diagnostic. 10 strategies + per-ch oracle. 14/14 test_threshold_tune.ax.");
  let gtt = make_findings_grid(sec_tt) in
  render_finding(gtt, "Optimal threshold = 15, not 10: +17 (3x S1640)", "S4: 792/2799 vs S0: 775/2799", "The threshold sweep reveals a non-monotonic curve: thr=3 (+5), thr=5 (+5), thr=10 (+6), thr=15 (+17 PEAK), thr=20 (+14), thr=30 (-20). Too little gating misses sparse contexts. Too much injects noise from unreliable neural estimates. Captures 17% of oracle gap (up from 6%). The sweet spot targets contexts with 10-15 observations -- enough for trigrams to be partially informative but not reliable.", "S1641");
  render_finding(gtt, "Bigram matches neural at sparse: gain is SMOOTHING", "S9(bigram): 782 (+7) vs S3(neural): 781 (+6) -- margin 1", "When trigram count < 10, bigram count distributions match neural distributions (margin of 1 position out of 2799, statistically indistinguishable). The gain from sparse gating does NOT require neural embedding generalization -- count table SMOOTHING via bigram suffices. Bigrams have larger bins (q^2 vs q^3 contexts), so more data per bin. The mechanism is data pooling: when trigram evidence is sparse, pooling into the coarser bigram provides better estimates. Neural happens to give similar results because both are filling the same statistical gap.", "S1641");
  render_finding(gtt, "Adaptive per-ch thresholds LOSE", "S7(q): 757 (-18), S8(2q): 706 (-69)", "Per-channel thresholds proportional to modulus (q or 2q) HURT badly. At q=49 threshold, b-channel gets gated 740/2799 times (26% of positions), injecting too much noise. The uniform threshold works because it targets a consistent severity of sparsity regardless of channel. Channel-specific tuning overreacts to high-q channels where even moderate trigram counts are sufficient.", "S1641");
  render_finding(gtt, "Non-monotonic: narrow sweet spot around 15", "Rise: 0 to +17. Crash: -20 at thr=30", "The gain curve has a sharp peak. Below threshold 10: under-gating (missing sparse positions that need smoothing -- thr=10 gains only +6 vs +17 at thr=15). Above 20: over-gating (replacing reliable count evidence with less reliable smoothed estimates). At 30: net negative because most gated positions have adequate trigram data that count handles better. The optimal threshold 15 corresponds to approximately 1-2 observations per residue bin on average.", "S1641");
  dom_append(sec_tt, gtt);
  dom_append(dom_body(), sec_tt)
)
